window.DOCS_INDEX = [{"t": "Bud Decision Studio documentation", "s": "Get started", "u": "index.html", "h": "", "x": "Use the app. Install the studio Windows, macOS and Linux, and what your computer needs.  Ask your first question Load a model, describe a situation and read the answer.  Save it as a template Reuse the same questions with new details each time.  Review what was decided Every decision, what the model saw, and what needed a person.  Choose a model The eleven models, what each is good at and the memory it needs.  Teach it your decisions Train a model on your own examples; keep it only if it got better.  Build with the API. Call the studio from your code Send a situation and questions, act on the ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Bud Decision Studio documentation", "s": "Get started", "u": "index.html#everything-else", "h": "Everything else", "x": "Concepts. Decision models. The six question types. Probabilities, certainty and acting. Templates and versions. History and privacy. Fine-tuning and its safeguards. Guides. Build a support-triage template. Improve a template safely. Teach a model your own decisions. Let an agent choose its next action. Decide about images, audio and video. Developers. Architecture. Run from source. Add a model. Th", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#what-your-computer-needs", "h": "What your computer needs", "x": "Windows Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit (Intel or AMD processor). macOS macOS 12.3 or later on Apple Silicon (M1 or newer). Intel Macs are not supported, because current PyTorch builds no longer run on them.  Linux 64-bit Intel or AMD, or ARM64 (including the NVIDIA GB10 in a DGX Spark). The app's window needs WebKitGTK 4.1, which the .deb and .rpm packages install for you.  A GPU is optional. Setup find", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#install-with-one-command", "h": "Install with one command", "x": "The one-line installers fetch the latest release, install it in the usual place for your system and open the app. They also avoid the warnings that macOS and Windows show for apps downloaded through a browser.  Windows macOS Linux Run the command in PowerShell. It installs the app for your user only (no administrator rights are needed), adds it to the Start menu and opens it.  Run the command in T", "k": "sudo", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#download-the-installer-yourself", "h": "Download the installer yourself", "x": "Every release is on the releases page. For version 0.3.0:  Windows macOS Linux Bud.Decision.Studio_0.3.0_x64-setup.exe 15.1 MB. Bud.Decision.Studio_0.3.0_x64_en-US.msi 22.0 MB. Run either one. The app is added to the Start menu. The installers are not signed yet, so Windows SmartScreen may warn you: choose More info, then Run anyway.  Bud.Decision.Studio_0.3.0_aarch64.dmg 21.2 MB. Open the disk im", "k": "_aarch64.AppImage _arm64.deb aarch64 arm64", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#the-first-launch", "h": "The first launch", "x": "The first time the app opens, it walks you through four short screens. Later launches go straight to the studio", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#1-it-checks-your-computer", "h": "1. It checks your computer", "x": "Setup lists your system, its graphics and processor, how much memory each has, and the free disk space. Choose Continue.  Fig. 1 The welcome screen on an NVIDIA GB10: setup has found the GPU, the processor and 96 GB of free disk", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#2-you-choose-where-models-run", "h": "2. You choose where models run", "x": "The recommended choice is selected: the GPU when there is one. Each choice says how fast it is and how much it downloads. Choose Install.  Fig. 2 Where should models run? Here the GPU engine is PyTorch for CUDA 13.0, about 4.4 GB; the processor-only engine is about 1.5 GB", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#3-it-installs-the-engine", "h": "3. It installs the engine", "x": "Setup prepares Python, installs the PyTorch build for your choice and the model libraries, then checks that the device works. This takes a few minutes on a fast connection; keep the app open until it finishes. Details shows the full log. If a step fails, Try again starts it again. Setup never changes anything outside the app's own folder.  Fig. 3 Installing the engine. Each step is ticked off as i", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#4-you-choose-which-models-to-download", "h": "4. You choose which models to download", "x": "When setup is done, choose Open Bud Decision Studio. The studio opens with a list of models, two of them ticked because they suit your computer. Tick the ones you want and choose Download. They download one at a time, smallest first, in the background, so you can start as soon as the first is ready. Not now skips this step; the Models page offers the same list later.  Fig. 4 The model list. On fir", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#where-your-files-are", "h": "Where your files are", "x": "The app keeps everything it creates in one folder per user:  Windows %APPDATA%\\ai.bud.decisionstudio. macOS ~/Library/Application Support/ai.bud.decisionstudio. Linux ~/.local/share/ai.bud.decisionstudio. Inside it:  engine-env/ the engine: Python, PyTorch and the model libraries. data/studio.db History, templates and their versions, test examples and settings, in one SQLite database. data/blobs/,", "k": "HF_HOME sensitive", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#updating", "h": "Updating", "x": "Install the new version the same way you installed the first one: run the one-line command again, or install the new file over the old one. Your engine, models, templates and History stay where they are, and setup does not run again.  When a new version changes the History database, the studio copies the database to data/backups/ before it upgrades it. A database written by a newer version than th", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#uninstalling", "h": "Uninstalling", "x": "Windows macOS Linux Open Settings, then Apps, find Bud Decision Studio and choose Uninstall.  Quit the app and drag it from Applications to the Trash.  Remove the package you installed. For the AppImage, delete the file and its menu entry.  Removing the app leaves your data and models in place, so a reinstall picks up where you left off. To remove them too:  Delete the app's data folder (see Where", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Installation", "s": "Get started", "u": "install.html#running-from-source-instead", "h": "Running from source instead", "x": "Developers can run the studio from a checkout of the repository, without the desktop app:  install.sh detects your hardware, asks where models run and installs everything into .venv, exactly as setup does in the app. A source checkout keeps its data in ./data instead of the app's folder. Run from source has the details", "k": "install.sh", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#before-you-start", "h": "Before you start", "x": "Install the app and download one model. Laya is a good first choice: it is a 0.8 GB download, needs about 1.2 GB of memory, runs on any computer and answers English questions in milliseconds on a GPU. If you skipped the model list on first launch, open Models, select Laya and choose Download.  A decision model does not write text. You give it a situation (an email, a ticket, a log line, some JSON)", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#ask-your-first-question", "h": "Ask your first question", "x": "Open Playground from the sidebar. It opens on an example: a customer message about a double charge, and three questions about it.  Check the model. The model button at the top left shows Laya. Choose it (or press Ctrl L, ⌘ L on a Mac) to pick another downloaded model. A model that is not loaded yet loads the first time you use it.  Read the situation. Under State is the message the model will judg", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#read-the-answer", "h": "Read the answer", "x": "Each answer is a figure:  The answer in large type with how likely the model thinks it is: billing 98%.  Every option on the same 0 to 100% scale, so you can see what came second and by how much.  A plain-language label at the top right: Very sure (90% or more), Fairly sure (75% or more), Leaning (55% or more) or Unsure.  The verdict in the caption: Act automatically when the answer is at least as", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#make-it-your-own", "h": "Make it your own", "x": "Press New to start a blank decision. Paste your own text under State, then choose a kind of answer under Create new Decision:  Fig. 2 The six kinds of question. Each answer comes back as probabilities over the options you write. Write the question as a sentence and add its options. For Pick one, add a short description to an option when its name alone could be misread (for example, billing with \"i", "k": "billing", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#save-it-as-a-template", "h": "Save it as a template", "x": "Once the questions work, keep them. A template is a decision you can reuse: its questions, its default model and its settings, under a short id your code can call. Every save is a numbered version.  Choose Save as template at the bottom of the left column.  Name it. Type Ticket routing. The id, used in code, fills in as ticket-routing.  Choose Save template.  Fig. 3 Save as template keeps the ques", "k": "ticket-routing", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#find-it-in-history", "h": "Find it in History", "x": "Every decision is kept on your computer, wherever it came from: the Playground, your code, an SDK or curl. Choose Saved to History above an answer to open that decision, or open History from the sidebar to see them all. You can filter by template, model, result and time, see which decisions asked a human, label the right answers and rerun a decision on another model.  Each template also has its ow", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#call-it-from-your-code", "h": "Call it from your code", "x": "While the app is open, the studio listens at http://127.0.0.1:8420 on your computer (the API page shows the exact address). No key is needed from the same machine. Send the template's id and a new situation.  The response is the decision as it was kept, with an id you can look up later. The response in the example is a real one from Laya, shortened to the fields that matter here.  Read it the same", "k": "act false id needs_review technical x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "Quickstart", "s": "Get started", "u": "quickstart.html#where-to-go-next", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Playground: every control, including comparing models and testing option order.  Templates: variables, versions, aliases and comparing two versions on real traffic.  Evaluate: measure a model on your own labelled examples and pick a safe act threshold.  Call the studio from your code: the full path from a template to production code.  API reference: every endpoint, field and error", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The app at a glance", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/index.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The app at a glance", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/index.html#the-window", "h": "The window", "x": "Fig. 1 The studio with the Playground open. The sidebar lists the pages and the loaded models; the title bar names the page; the status bar shows where models run, memory, and the API address. The sidebar lists the pages in three groups:  (top) Playground, Templates, Models, Evaluate, Train. Developer History, API. Help Learn, System. Next to Models is the number of models downloaded to this compu", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The app at a glance", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/index.html#the-pages", "h": "The pages", "x": "Playground Describe a situation, ask questions about it, and read every answer with its probabilities. Compare models, test option order, and save a decision as a template.  Templates Keep decisions you reuse: their questions, variables, default model and settings, with every change saved as a numbered version. Compare two versions on real decisions.  Models See the eleven open models, what each i", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The app at a glance", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/index.html#a-typical-session", "h": "A typical session", "x": "Open Models and load the model you want (or let the Playground load it when you press Decide ).  In the Playground, describe the situation, add questions and press Decide.  When the questions work, choose Save as template so your code can call them by name.  Call the template from your code. Each call appears in History, where you check the decisions that asked a human and label the right answers.", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The app at a glance", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/index.html#keyboard-shortcuts", "h": "Keyboard shortcuts", "x": "Ctrl Enter Playground Decide. Ctrl L Playground Open the model list to choose or load a model. Enter, Esc Model list Choose the highlighted model, or close the list. ↑ ↓ Models table Move between models. Esc Menus and dialogs Close. On a Mac, ⌘ works in place of Ctrl", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The app at a glance", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/index.html#in-a-browser", "h": "In a browser", "x": "The studio's pages are served by the studio itself, so while the app is open you can also use it in any browser on the same computer at http://127.0.0.1:8420 (the status bar shows the exact address). A studio run from source has no app window at all: open that address in a browser.  The Playground and Evaluate remember what you were working on in the window or browser you used, so a draft survives", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#the-layout", "h": "The layout", "x": "Fig. 1 The Playground with an answer. Left: the model, the situation ( State) and the Questions. Right: the answers, one figure per question, with the Answers, JSON and Code tabs above them. The left column is the decision you are building; the right column is what the model answered. The row at the top of the left column has the model button, the Templates menu, New and Examples. Decide is pinned", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#choose-a-model", "h": "Choose a model", "x": "The model button shows the model in use, whether it is loaded, its size and the memory it needs. Choose it, or press Ctrl L, to open the model list.  Fig. 2 The model list. Type to filter, sort by Ready first, Size or Name, and press Enter to choose. Each row shows the maker, the size, the memory needed and what the model reads. Choosing a model that is not loaded loads it with its default setting", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#describe-the-situation", "h": "Describe the situation", "x": "Under State, type or paste what you want judged: an email, a support ticket, a review, a log line, a policy and a request. Use Text for prose and JSON for structured data, such as a ticket with its customer's plan:  Below the box, the studio estimates the length in tokens (about three quarters of a word each) and shows how much the model reads. Laya reads 512 tokens; most larger models read 8,192.", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#ask-questions", "h": "Ask questions", "x": "Under Create new Decision, choose the kind of answer you want. You can add as many questions as the model allows; it answers them all at once, in one pass.  Fig. 3 The six kinds of question. Pick one a list of options a probability for every option; exactly one is the answer. Rate on a scale 2 to 10 ordered levels, lowest first a probability for every level, plus an average position that can land ", "k": "billing choice department noul score", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#decide-and-read-the-answers", "h": "Decide and read the answers", "x": "Press Decide or Ctrl Enter. If the model is not loaded, it loads first and the answers area shows its progress. While the model reads, each question card lights up in turn.  The line above the answers records the run: the model, how many questions it answered and how long it took, the number of tokens it read, the template and version if you used one, and Saved to History, which opens the decision", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#the-json-and-code-tabs", "h": "The JSON and Code tabs", "x": "JSON shows the exact request the Playground sent to POST /v1/studio/decisions and the response it received, as expandable trees. Code writes the same decision as a program you can copy:  Fig. 5 The Code tab. Choose a language (curl, Python, the TypeSafe Python SDK, JavaScript or the TypeSafe JavaScript SDK) and an API format. The default format is TypeSafe's Jev API, which the official SDKs speak.", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#decision-settings", "h": "Decision settings", "x": "The sliders button above the answers opens the decision settings.  Fig. 6 Decision settings: when to act, calibration, History, comparing models and testing option order. Act threshold The rule for trusting an answer: at or above this probability, the verdict is Act automatically; below it, Ask a human. From 50% to 99%; 90% by default.  Temperature A calibration temperature: one number that makes ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#compare-models", "h": "Compare models", "x": "Load two or more models, turn on Ask every loaded model and press Compare. Each model runs in its own process, so they answer in parallel.  Fig. 7 Julia 1 and Laya on a German message. They agree on the team but not on the customer's mood or whether it needs fixing today, so the chips say 1 of 2 agree. Each row is a question; each column is a model with its answer, probability and time. Where mode", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#test-option-order", "h": "Test option order", "x": "Some models favour an option because of where it appears in the list. Test option order checks for this on your pick-one and put-in-order questions with three or more options: it runs the decision five times, the first in your order and the rest shuffled. Stable means the same answer every time. Changes with order means the answer depends on the order: treat it as unsure, reword the options, or tr", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#use-a-template", "h": "Use a template", "x": "A template is a saved decision your code can call by name. In the Playground, the Templates button lists your templates, All templates (including the starter templates) and, when no template is open, Save this decision as a template.  Opening a template changes the left column:  Fig. 8 The Support triage template open in the Playground. Its Variables form replaces the situation box: here a message", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Playground", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/playground.html#save-a-decision-as-a-template", "h": "Save a decision as a template", "x": "When a decision works, choose Save as template. Give it a name; the id your code will use fills in from the name (lower-case letters, digits, dashes and underscores), and you can change it. The template keeps the questions, the current model as its default model, and the current act threshold and temperature.  To give the situation a fixed shape, write placeholders in it before saving, such as:  E", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#the-library", "h": "The library", "x": "Open Templates from the sidebar.  Fig. 1 The library. Your templates first, then the Starter templates, grouped by use. Each card shows how many questions and variables the template has, its default model and when it was last used. Search templates filters by id, name and description.  Show archived includes templates you have archived.  New template offers Build it in the Playground (write and tr", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#ways-to-make-a-template", "h": "Ways to make a template", "x": "From the Playground. Build a decision, then choose Save as template. See Playground.  From a past decision. In History, open a decision's ... menu and choose Save as template.  From a definition. New template, then Paste a definition.  From a starter. Open it and choose Clone to edit.  From code. PUT /v1/studio/templates/{id} creates the template, and calling it again with changes saves the next v", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#a-templates-page", "h": "A template's page", "x": "Choose a template to open its page. The header shows its name, description and id, the latest version, any aliases (such as production: v2 ), and whether History keeps less than everything for it. On the right:  Open in Playground runs it with the variables form.  Edit opens it in the Playground with its questions unlocked, so you can try changes and Save version. Starter templates have Clone to e", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#overview", "h": "Overview", "x": "Fig. 2 The Overview of Support triage version 2: its five variables and four questions, its model and settings, the models that can run it, and the call to use it from code. The situation lists the template's variables, if it has any: their names, types, defaults and whether they are optional or sensitive. How they become the situation shows the state the variables are written into. A template wit", "k": "state support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#variables", "h": "Variables", "x": "Variables give the situation a fixed shape: callers fill in a message, a plan tier or a screenshot, and the template writes them into the situation in the same way every time.  text a string; optionally one of a fixed list ( enum ), with a maximum length. whole number, number a number, optionally between a minimum and a maximum. yes or no true or false. JSON any JSON value. a list of options the o", "k": "enum false true", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#history", "h": "History", "x": "The History tab lists the decisions made with this template, newest first, with the version each one used.  Fig. 3 Decisions made with Support triage. The strip at the top counts decisions per version, how many acted automatically and the typical time. Choose a decision to open it in History. Filter and label them in History opens the full History page filtered to this template, where you can labe", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#compare-versions", "h": "Compare versions", "x": "When a template has two or more versions, Compare versions shows how they behave on real decisions, so you can decide whether to switch.  Fig. 4 Version 1 against version 2. Version 2 added the account option and the wants_refund question and lowered the act threshold; callers keep working. Choose the two versions, optionally one model, and a time range ( All time, 7 days, 30 days ). The strip com", "k": "account wants_refund", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#test-examples", "h": "Test examples", "x": "Test examples are inputs with their right answers. Use them to check a new version, or another model, before switching.  Fig. 5 The Test examples tab: each example's input, its right answers and its tags. The quickest way to collect them is from real decisions: in History, label a decision's right answers, then choose Add to support-triage's test examples in its ... menu. Add example adds one by h", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#versions", "h": "Versions", "x": "Every save is a new version, and versions never change once saved.  Fig. 6 The version list: each version's change class, aliases, the note written when it was saved and a summary of what changed. For each version:  View shows it on the Overview tab.  Compare with 1 (the previous version) opens Compare versions.  Set alias points a name, such as production or staging, at this version. Code that ca", "k": "latest production staging", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#changing-a-template", "h": "Changing a template", "x": "Choose Edit. The Playground opens the latest version with its questions unlocked. Try your changes with Decide; these trial decisions are kept in History as drafts and do not count in the template's statistics. When you are happy, choose Save version, describe what changed, and the edits become the next version. Callers of the latest version use it at once; callers pinned to a version or an alias ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#what-history-keeps-for-a-template", "h": "What History keeps for a template", "x": ", then History for this template, sets two limits for every decision made with the template:  What is kept for each decision: Everything, Answers only (the answers, model and timing, but not the situation) or Nothing. Callers can keep less than this, never more.  Keep decisions for (days): empty uses the studio setting; 0 keeps them for ever", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/templates.html#archive-and-delete", "h": "Archive and delete", "x": "Archive hides a template from the library; code that calls it keeps working. Unarchive brings it back.  Delete removes the template: code that calls it gets \"template not found\". Its decisions stay in History with the exact version they used; delete them there if you want them gone", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#the-page", "h": "The page", "x": "Open History from the sidebar.  Fig. 1 History for the last 24 hours. The summary and the chart are on the left with the list below them; the selected decision is on the right, with its answers redrawn. Everything stays on your computer, in the studio's database. By default each decision is kept for 30 days, with the situation, the questions, the answers, the model and the timing. What History kee", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#find-decisions", "h": "Find decisions", "x": "The bar at the top filters the list, the summary and the chart together:  Live and Pause The list refreshes every few seconds while Live. Pause holds it still while you read.  Time range 1 hour, 24 hours, 7 days or All. Result Every result, Needs review (asked a human), Acted automatically, Failed, Labelled, Pinned. Model Every model, or one model. Template Every template, No template, one of your", "k": "curl", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#read-a-decision", "h": "Read a decision", "x": "Choose a decision in the list. The panel on the right shows its result, when it ran, its template and version (or \"Decision without a template\"), the model and the source, then four tabs.  Answers redraws every answer exactly as the Playground showed it, with the verdict each question got at the time.  Input shows the variables the caller sent (if the decision used a template) and What the model r", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#act-on-a-decision", "h": "Act on a decision", "x": "The buttons above the tabs:  Open in Playground loads the decision into the Playground: in its template, with the same variables and per-decision changes, if it used one; otherwise as a free decision with its situation and questions.  Rerun on runs the same input on a loaded model, the same one or another. The rerun is a new decision, marked Rerun, so you can compare the two.  Pin keeps the decisi", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#label-the-right-answers", "h": "Label the right answers", "x": "Under the answers, Label the right answers lists each question with the answers it allows. Choose the right one; the studio marks the model's answer right or wrong.  Fig. 5 Labelling a Support triage decision. Labels are kept with the decision. Labels feed Accuracy on labels when you compare template versions, and a labelled decision can become a test example in one step ( ..., then Add to templat", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#what-history-keeps", "h": "What History keeps", "x": "The gear button at the top right of the page opens the History settings.  Fig. 6 What History keeps, how long decisions are kept, and how much space History uses. What History keeps for each decision:  Everything the situation, the questions, the answers, the model and the timing (the default). Answers only the questions, answers, model and timing, but not the situation or any files. Nothing nothi", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/history.html#history-from-code", "h": "History from code", "x": "Everything on this page is also available through the API: list and filter decisions, fetch one with its input, add labels, rerun, export as JSON lines or CSV, and delete or redact decisions. Each response names the stored decision in the x-basal-decision-id header and says what was kept in x-basal-stored. See History, feedback and statistics", "k": "x-basal-decision-id x-basal-stored", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#the-page", "h": "The page", "x": "Open Models from the sidebar.  Fig. 1 The Models page. The table lists every model; the panel on the right describes the selected one, here Laya, which is loaded. The table has a row per model: its name and one-line summary, its size in parameters ( Size ), the memory it needs when loaded ( Needs ), what it reads ( Reads: text, images, audio, video), how its published results compare with Jev ( Ah", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#which-model-to-use", "h": "Which model to use", "x": "Julia 1 144M fast multilingual routing text. Laya Multilingual 322M decisions in 100+ languages text. Laya 421M English triage and guardrails; the most popular open decision model text. Laya Typed-Decisions 421M invoices, security and support workflows text. Kev 0.5B 0.5B learning how the architecture works text. GLiNER2.5 Decide 340M operational labels; fast on a processor text. Intern-Decision 4", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#read-about-a-model", "h": "Read about a model", "x": "The panel on the right has three tabs.  Overview describes the model in plain words, then lists the examples it was made for, the facts ( Parameters, Memory when loaded, Reads, Languages, Context, Download, License ), what it is Good for and what to Watch out for. Technical details has its architecture, base model, training, the question types it answers natively, how many options and questions it", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#download-a-model", "h": "Download a model", "x": "Select a model and choose Download, or choose Download models to pick several at once.  Fig. 6 Download models. Tick the models you want, or use Recommended or Everything that fits. The line at the bottom adds up the download and the free disk. Models download one at a time, smallest first, in the background. The Downloads button in the title bar shows the queue, the progress and the time left, an", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#load-and-eject", "h": "Load and eject", "x": "A model uses memory only while it is loaded. Choose Load, or simply use the model: the Playground and Evaluate load a model when they need it, and so do API requests that name a downloaded model (the System page can turn that off for API requests).  To choose how it runs, open the Load tab before loading. The settings depend on the model; most have:  Run on GPU or CPU for this model. A GPU is much", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#when-a-model-does-not-load", "h": "When a model does not load", "x": "The status changes to Failed to load and the buttons offer Retry and View log. The log is the model process's own output and usually names the problem: most often there was not enough free memory (eject other models or close other programs that use the GPU) or a file is missing (delete the model's files and download it again).  If a model loads but the System page marks it On the processor, the GP", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Models", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/models.html#remove-a-model", "h": "Remove a model", "x": "Open the model's Load tab and choose Delete files. The studio says how much disk this frees and keeps a base model that another downloaded model still uses. You can download the model again at any time", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html#why-evaluate", "h": "Why evaluate", "x": "A model's published results are measured on someone else's data. Before you let it act on its own, measure it on yours: a few dozen real examples with the right answers tell you more than any benchmark. Evaluate answers three questions:  How often is it right? Accuracy on your examples.  Can its percentages be trusted? When it says 80%, is it right about 80% of the time? This is called calibration", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html#set-up-a-run", "h": "Set up a run", "x": "The left column has three steps.  1. The question. Write one question, as in the Playground. Evaluate takes Pick one, Rate on a scale and Yes or no questions.  2. Examples. One example per line, with the right answer after it. Sample loads 24 support messages, each labelled with the team that should handle it. Open file reads a file from your computer; Edit as text shows the examples as text you c", "k": "answer correct false label no state text true yes", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html#read-the-results", "h": "Read the results", "x": "The table ranks the models:  Right the share of scored examples the model answered correctly. Calibration error how far its stated certainty is from how often it is actually right, on average. Lower is better; 0 is perfectly honest.  Confident mistakes answers given with 90% or more that were wrong: the most dangerous kind of mistake. Typical time the median time per decision. While the run is goi", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html#use-the-recommendations", "h": "Use the recommendations", "x": "Under the charts, Evaluate recommends two settings for the selected model.  Act threshold. The lowest threshold at which the answers the model acts on are wrong less often than your Error budget (1%, 2%, 5%, 10% or 20%; 5% by default), with how many examples that automates and at what accuracy. Use this threshold makes it the Playground's act threshold. The recommendation stays within 50% to 99%, ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html#check-every-example", "h": "Check every example", "x": "Every example lists each example, its right answer and each model's answer with its probability, marked right or wrong. Mistakes shows only the examples some model got wrong; Confident mistakes only those answered wrongly with 90% or more. The table shows the first 400 rows.  Export CSV downloads every example with each model's answer and probability, as bud-decision-results.csv", "k": "bud-decision-results.csv", "f": ""}, {"t": "Evaluate", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/evaluate.html#notes", "h": "Notes", "x": "Evaluate remembers your question and examples in this window.  Every decision a run makes is kept in History, marked Evaluate, so you can open any of them later. The Playground's Keep this decision in History switch does not apply here; to keep nothing from a run, set What History keeps to Nothing in History's settings first.  To check a template against its own test examples, or two versions of a", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#before-you-start", "h": "Before you start", "x": "Training runs on this computer's GPU and needs no account or upload: your examples never leave the computer.  A GPU. An NVIDIA RTX 30 series or newer (including the DGX Spark and other GB10 machines) or an Apple M2 or newer works. Intel Arc and Core Ultra graphics, AMD on Linux, older NVIDIA cards and the Apple M1 are experimental: the page offers Try training on this GPU to switch them on. On a c", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#add-your-examples", "h": "Add your examples", "x": "Drop a file on the page, choose Choose a file, or choose Try it with an example file to use 420 made-up IT support tickets, each labelled with the team that should handle it and whether it is urgent. What should the file look like? shows a three-row example.  The studio reads CSV, tab-separated text, JSON lines and the studio's own training format, which is described in Teach a model your own deci", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#check-what-it-will-learn", "h": "Check what it will learn", "x": "Fig. 2 The example file reviewed: two questions, how often each answer appears, the recommended model with its time estimate, and the name the new model will have. Each question is shown with how many examples answer it and how often each answer appears. The text box is the question's instruction, the sentence the model reads: reword it the way you would ask it. Problems are listed under the quest", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#while-it-learns", "h": "While it learns", "x": "Fig. 4 Training in progress: the stage, the time left, and the practice score on examples the model isn't learning from, rising from where it started. The page shows what it is doing, how far it has got and about how long is left. The chart is the practice score: how often the model is right on a set of your examples it is not learning from, measured every few minutes, against the dashed line wher", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#the-result", "h": "The result", "x": "Fig. 5 Laya taught the example file, and kept: right on all 126 answers it never saw, up from 76%; no change on general decisions; one ticket it used to get wrong, with the answers before and now. The large number is how often the new model is right on examples it never saw during training, with the original model's score beside it. Under it:  General decisions it already knew. The same general qu", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#when-the-original-stays", "h": "When the original stays", "x": "If the new version isn't kept, the page says why in one sentence, and the original model is unchanged. Teach another model instead starts the same examples on the next model that can learn them, with its time estimate. If the original model already gets almost all of your examples right, the page says so: there was little left to learn", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Train", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/train.html#trained-models", "h": "Trained models", "x": "Fig. 8 Trained models listed on the Train page with their held-out scores before and after, and the most recent trainings. A trained model is a model like the others: it appears in the Playground's model list, on the Models page with a Fine-tuned badge, in Evaluate, and to your code under its own id (the Playground's Code tab shows it). It loads the original model and adds a small file of changes,", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#the-api-page", "h": "The API page", "x": "Open API from the sidebar. It has everything you need to call the studio from your own code.  Fig. 1 The API page: the server's status and address at the top, the endpoints and a quick-start example on the left, and the reference on the right. The top section shows that the server is Running and its Base URL (copy it with the button beside it). It also covers:  Interactive reference Opens /docs on", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY decision jev-latest latency_ms laya media multi number rank temperature top_probability x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#calls-from-other-computers", "h": "Calls from other computers", "x": "The studio listens only on your own computer, and other websites open in your browser cannot send it requests. To serve other machines, run the studio from source with a key.  Clients then send Authorization: Bearer <key>. Requests that change things (loading, downloading or deleting models) must also send the header X-Basal-Client: 1", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#learn", "h": "Learn", "x": "Open Learn from the sidebar for a ten-minute introduction to decision models, written for people who have not used one before.  Fig. 2 Learn. The introduction is on the left; on the right, a decision about a support message runs live on the loaded model. It covers:  How to read a figure: the answer, every option on one scale, and the caption.  How is this different from a chatbot? A decision model", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#system", "h": "System", "x": "Open System from the sidebar to see where models run and how memory is used.  Fig. 3 The System page on an NVIDIA GB10, with Laya loaded. Its GPU and processor share one pool of memory, most of it used here by other programs", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#where-models-run", "h": "Where models run", "x": "Run models on switches between the GPU and the processor (CPU). It applies to models you load from now on; loaded models keep running where they are. The choices are the ones the installed engine supports.  Installed engine says which PyTorch build is installed and when. To run models on a different kind of processor (for example, after adding a GPU), the engine must be reinstalled: in the desktop", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#memory-and-load", "h": "Memory and load", "x": "The figures at the top show what models run on, how busy it is (it jumps while a model answers), its temperature and power where the GPU reports them, and the free space on the disk that holds the models folder.  Memory shows how much memory is in use and how much is available for more models, split into loaded models (violet), other programs (grey) and free. On computers whose GPU and processor s", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#memory-management", "h": "Memory management", "x": "Eject models that have not been used for Never (keep them loaded), 15 minutes, 1 hour or 4 hours. Frees memory for other programs automatically; an ejected model loads again the next time it is used.  Load models on demand On: when an API request names a downloaded model that is not loaded, the studio loads it and then answers; the request waits. Off: such requests are refused", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API, Learn and System", "s": "User manual", "u": "manual/system.html#training", "h": "Training", "x": "Allow experimental training lets the Train page use GPUs that work but haven't been tested for training as fully: Intel Arc and Core Ultra graphics, AMD on Linux, NVIDIA RTX 20 series and Apple M1. On NVIDIA RTX 30 series or newer and Apple M2 or newer, training is always on. The Train page offers the same switch as Try training on this GPU.  Fig. 4 The Training setting at the end of the System pa", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#what-a-decision-model-does", "h": "What a decision model does", "x": "You give the model two things:  A situation, called the state: an email, a support ticket, a log line, a JSON record, or the steps an agent has taken so far.  Typed questions about it. Each question says what kind of answer it wants (pick one option, yes or no, a point on a scale) and lists the answers you allow.  The model reads both in one pass and returns, for every question, a probability for ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#how-it-differs-from-a-chat-model", "h": "How it differs from a chat model", "x": "A chat model (an LLM you prompt for text) can answer the same questions, but you get prose back and have to parse it. A decision model is built for one job, and that changes what you can rely on.  Output Free text you parse A probability for every allowed answer. Answers outside your options Possible Impossible: it can only score the options you gave. How sure it is Not reported, or reported in wo", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#the-situation", "h": "The situation", "x": "The state can be plain text, a JSON object or a JSON array. The studio turns JSON into readable text before the model sees it: field names become labels, nested fields are indented, and list items get a dash. Naming fields well ( last_observation, not obs) therefore helps the model.  The example on the right is an agent's state from the agent guide, and the text the model actually read. Each model", "k": "last_observation obs state_may_be_truncated", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#the-eleven-models", "h": "The eleven models", "x": "Every model below speaks the same request format and returns answers in the same shape, so you can switch models without changing code. They differ in size, speed, what they read and how many options they handle.  Julia 1 julia-1 144M 0.8 GB text 20 8,192 Fast multilingual routing. Laya Multilingual laya-multilingual 322M 1.0 GB text 20 1,024 Decisions in 100+ languages. Laya laya 421M 1.2 GB text", "k": "clm-v0.1-8b gliner2.5-decide intern-decision-4b jev-omni julia-1 kev-0.5b kev-4b laya laya-multilingual laya-typed-decisions lev", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#limits-are-checked-before-the-model-runs", "h": "Limits are checked before the model runs", "x": "The studio knows each model's limits and checks your request against the model that will answer, before loading or running anything. A request the model cannot handle gets one 400 model_incompatible error that lists every problem: an unsupported question type, too many options, too many questions, or media the model cannot read.  The example on the right sends an image to Laya, which reads text on", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#how-the-studio-runs-a-model", "h": "How the studio runs a model", "x": "Each loaded model runs in its own process, on the GPU or on the processor. Ejecting a model ends its process, which is the only way to give every byte of memory back, and a crash in one model cannot take down the studio or the others.  Loading. A model must be downloaded and loaded before it answers. A request for a model that is downloaded but not loaded loads it first, which can take from a few ", "k": "act auto certainty confidence default jev-1.13 jev-latest model", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decision models", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/decision-models.html#when-to-use-one", "h": "When to use one", "x": "Decision models fit work where the answer is one of a known set and you want a number you can set rules on:  routing tickets, emails and leads to a team;  rating urgency, risk, sentiment or quality on a scale;  checking a rule: is this a refund request, does this break the policy, is this a prompt injection;  choosing an agent's next action or tool from a list;  labelling records with every tag th", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#the-types-at-a-glance", "h": "The types at a glance", "x": "The app names each type in plain words; the API uses the type value in the second column.  Pick one choice Named options, with optional descriptions A probability per option, and the winner The winning option's name. Rate on a scale score Ordered levels, lowest first A probability per level, and an average position The most likely level's key: \"0\", \"1\", …. Yes or no noul A statement to judge The p", "k": "choice decision multi noul number rank score type", "f": ""}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#ask-all-six-at-once", "h": "Ask all six at once", "x": "One request can mix every type, and all questions are answered in the same pass. The request on the right asks six questions about one support ticket. The response is shortened to each answer's decision and certainty; the sections below show each answer in full.  Every answer also carries:  decision The answer in one canonical value, the same vocabulary you use to label answers and filter History.", "k": "act adhoc certainty decision extended extra multi origin template top_probability", "f": "act certainty decision origin top_probability"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#pick-one-choice", "h": "Pick one: choice", "x": "The model picks exactly one option. Give each option a short name, which is what your code compares against, and a description when the name alone is ambiguous; a description often matters more to the model than the name. criteria can also be a plain list of names.  choice The winning option.  probabilities One probability per option, adding up to 1.  confidence TypeSafe's measure: (top − 1/K) / (", "k": "choice confidence criteria probabilities", "f": "choice confidence probabilities"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#rate-on-a-scale-score", "h": "Rate on a scale: score", "x": "Levels are ordered from lowest to highest. The answer gives a probability for each level, keyed \"0\", \"1\" and so on, and an average position: score 2.11 here means \"today, leaning towards blocking\". Use decision (the single most likely level) when you need one level, and score when you sort or threshold.  score The expected level: the average of the level numbers, weighted by probability.  legend Y", "k": "confidence decision legend score", "f": "confidence decision legend score"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#yes-or-no-noul", "h": "Yes or no: noul", "x": "The name comes from TypeSafe's API. Write the instruction as a statement or a question that is clearly true or false. noul is the probability of yes; decision is \"yes\" from 0.5 upwards. You can describe what yes and no mean with \"criteria\": {\"true\": \"…\", \"false\": \"…\"}.  noul The probability of yes.  probabilities false and true.  decision \"yes\" or \"no\"", "k": "decision false noul probabilities true", "f": "decision noul probabilities"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#pick-any-multi", "h": "Pick any: multi", "x": "Each option is judged on its own as a yes-or-no question, so any number of options can apply, including none. An option is selected when its probability reaches threshold (0.5 unless you set it). A multi question takes 1 to 64 options, and each option counts as one question towards the model's question limit.  threshold The cut-off for selecting an option, between 0 and 1. A per-decision override ", "k": "certainty multi probabilities selected threshold", "f": "certainty probabilities selected threshold"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#put-in-order-rank", "h": "Put in order: rank", "x": "A rank question is a choice whose options come back ordered from most to least likely. Use it when you will try options in turn: the reply to send first, the fix to try first, the candidate to show first. It needs at least two options.  ranking Every option, most likely first.  choice, decision The first option.  confidence As for choice", "k": "choice confidence decision rank ranking", "f": "choice confidence ranking"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#estimate-a-number-number", "h": "Estimate a number: number", "x": "You list the values the number can take, 2 to 64 of them, as a list or as {\"30\": \"half an hour\"} with descriptions. The model scores each value, and the answer reports the expected value, the single most likely value and the range that holds the central 80% of the probability. Add unit to label the values.  estimate The expected value.  most_likely The value with the highest probability; also deci", "k": "decision estimate most_likely number range unit", "f": "estimate most_likely range unit"}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#labels-use-the-same-values", "h": "Labels use the same values", "x": "When you record the right answer for a decision (feedback), add a test example or filter History by an answer, you use the same values as decision. A few other spellings are accepted on input and stored in the canonical form.  choice an option name none it equals choice. noul \"yes\" or \"no\" true, false, \"true\", \"false\" it equals decision. score a level key, \"0\" to \"n-1\" the level number, or the exa", "k": "choice decision false multi noul number range rank score true", "f": ""}, {"t": "The six question types", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/question-types.html#choosing-a-type", "h": "Choosing a type", "x": "One right answer from a fixed list: Pick one. If the list has more than about 20 entries, check the model's option limit, or use Lev or CLM 8B.  Several labels can be true at once: Pick any, not several yes-or-no questions; the threshold and certainty then work across the set.  An ordered judgement (urgency, severity, quality): Rate on a scale, so neighbouring levels count as near misses rather th", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html#probabilities", "h": "Probabilities", "x": "For every question, the model returns a probability for each answer you allowed. For a choice, score, rank or number question they add up to 1: 0.95 on billing leaves 0.05 for every other team together. For a multi question each option has its own probability that it applies, so they do not add up to anything in particular.  A probability is only useful if it means what it says. A model is well ca", "k": "billing choice multi number rank score", "f": ""}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html#calibration-temperature", "h": "Calibration temperature", "x": "The calibration temperature T reshapes every probability as p' ∝ p^(1/T), then rescales them to add up to 1 again:  T = 1 changes nothing.  T above 1 softens the answer: probabilities move towards each other, and the model sounds less sure.  T below 1 sharpens it: the leading answer gets more of the probability.  The temperature never changes which answer wins, only how sure the answer sounds. The", "k": "raw_probabilities technical", "f": ""}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html#certainty", "h": "Certainty", "x": "Certainty is the single number the studio uses to decide whether an answer can be acted on:  for choice, score, noul, rank and number, it is the probability of the winning answer ( top_probability );  for multi, it is the certainty of the weakest call: for each option, max(p, 1 − p), and the smallest of those. A multi answer is only as certain as its least certain option.  Certainty is not the sam", "k": "certainty choice confidence multi noul number rank score top_probability", "f": ""}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html#acting-automatically", "h": "Acting automatically", "x": "You choose an act threshold between 0 and 1. Each answer gets act: true when its certainty is at least the threshold, and act: false otherwise. The decision as a whole:  act true only when every answer acts.  needs_review The keys of the answers that did not act, in question order.  That gives a simple shape for code: act on what is certain, send the rest to a person. The threshold is the line bet", "k": "act answers.next_action.act needs_review true", "f": "act needs_review"}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html#where-the-settings-come-from", "h": "Where the settings come from", "x": "The act threshold and the temperature can be set in several places: per request, per template, per model inside a template, and per question in each of those. For every question and every setting, the studio takes the first value it finds in this order:  1 The request's per-question value: settings.questions.<key> request.questions. 2 The request: settings request. 3 The template, for this model a", "k": "churn_risk multi multi_threshold request request.questions settings settings.sources sources studio template template.questions threshold urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Probabilities, certainty and acting", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/acting.html#choosing-a-threshold", "h": "Choosing a threshold", "x": "A good threshold depends on the cost of a wrong automatic action against the cost of a person's time. Two tools help:  Evaluate, in the app, runs a question over labelled examples and recommends the threshold that keeps mistakes under the error rate you choose, showing how much work it would automate. See Evaluate.  What-if statistics recompute the gate over decisions already in History, from thei", "k": "act_rate urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#what-a-template-holds", "h": "What a template holds", "x": "Callers send a template's id and the values of its variables; the template supplies everything else. The response on the right is the support-triage template used throughout these docs, at version 2.  id The name used in code, chosen once and never changed: lowercase letters, digits, - and _.  name, description, metadata For people. Changing them never creates a version.  variables The values call", "k": "aliases audio description extensions id image latest metadata modalities model name note null production questions retention_days settings state storage text variables version video", "f": "aliases extensions id modalities model name questions settings state storage variables version"}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#variables", "h": "Variables", "x": "A variable is a typed value the caller sends with each decision. Types are strict: \"42\" is not an integer, and a typo in a variable name is reported rather than ignored, with a suggestion for the name you probably meant. Every problem in a request is reported at once.  string text min_length, max_length (default 20,000, at most 200,000), pattern, enum, format ( date or date-time). integer a whole ", "k": "audio boolean date date-time default description enum example false format image integer json max_bytes max_items max_length maximum min_items min_length minimum null number options pattern required schema sensitive string true trusted type video", "f": "default description example required sensitive trusted"}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#variables-in-question-text", "h": "Variables in question text", "x": "A variable can appear in a question's instructions or in option descriptions, as in the urgency question above. Variables with a closed set of values ( string with enum, integer, number, boolean, string with format) are always allowed there. A free-text string or json variable is refused when the version is saved, unless you declare it trusted: true: text in a question changes what the question as", "k": "boolean enum format integer json number string urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#how-variables-become-the-situation", "h": "How variables become the situation", "x": "Substitution is deliberately simple: {{name}} is replaced by the value, in one pass, with no expressions, conditions or loops. A customer who types {{account_tier}} in a message has it read literally.  In a JSON state, a string that is exactly one placeholder takes the value with its type: numbers stay numbers, objects stay objects.  A placeholder inside longer text, and every placeholder in a tex", "k": "customer_email open_invoices rendered_state urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#options-from-a-variable", "h": "Options from a variable", "x": "A question's whole option list can come from an options variable: write \"criteria\": \"{{actions}}\" on a choice, multi or rank question. Callers then send the options with each decision. This suits lists that change every call, such as the actions an agent can take right now; the agent guide builds one.  Answers whose options came from a variable carry dynamic_options: true. Statistics leave them ou", "k": "choice multi options rank", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#versions", "h": "Versions", "x": "Every change to what a template decides creates a new, numbered version: 1, 2, 3, with no gaps. A version never changes once saved. Each decision records the exact version it used, so History can always show the questions behind an answer and compare versions fairly.  What creates a version: a change to modalities, variables, state, questions, model, settings or extensions, whether sent with PUT, ", "k": "added base_version breaking breaking_for_callers description extended extensions identical incomparable metadata modalities model name options_changed PATCH PUT questions removed retention_days settings settings_only state storage text_changed variables wording", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#calling-a-version", "h": "Calling a version", "x": "There is one way to name a template in a request, a query or History:  support-triage The latest version when the request arrives. support-triage@3 Exactly version 3. support-triage@production Whichever version the production alias points at. An alias is a name you point at a version, and move later. Code that calls support-triage@production keeps working, unchanged, while you promote version 4 to", "k": "latest latest_callers_7d production support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#extensions", "h": "Extensions", "x": "A template can let callers adapt a single decision without changing the template. Its extensions field says what is allowed:  questions extensions.questions is true (the default), up to max_questions (default 16) Extra questions with new keys, asked alongside the template's.  add_options The question is listed in extensions.options New options appended to a choice, multi, rank or number question. ", "k": "add_options choice churn_risk extended extensions extensions.options extensions.questions extensions.skip extra finance_ops max_questions multi number origin production questions rank score skip template true", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#starter-templates", "h": "Starter templates", "x": "The studio comes with a read-only starter template for every Playground example, named builtin/<example>: builtin/support, builtin/agent-browser, builtin/image and so on. They take a whole situation instead of variables. Call them as they are, or clone one and make it yours; in the app, open it and choose Clone to edit. When a studio release changes a starter template, it gains a new version; your", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates and versions", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/templates.html#archiving-and-deleting", "h": "Archiving and deleting", "x": "Archive hides a template from the library and keeps it callable, so code that uses it keeps working; its decisions carry a template_archived warning. New versions are refused until you unarchive it.  Delete is the only change that breaks callers: they get template_not_found. By default the versions that History refers to stay readable, so past decisions still show their questions; deleting with hi", "k": "template_archived template_not_found", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#what-is-recorded", "h": "What is recorded", "x": "History is one database on your computer ( studio.db in the studio's data folder). It records decisions from every entry point: the Playground, the studio API ( /v1/studio ), TypeSafe's API ( /v1/systemone ), the OpenRouter and Vercel formats, Compare and Evaluate. A decision made by your code looks the same in History as one made in the app.  At the default level, full, a decision keeps:  the sit", "k": "full metadata studio.db x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#storage-levels", "h": "Storage levels", "x": "Each decision is stored at one of three levels:  full Everything above Review, labels, statistics, rerun, test examples. answers_only Everything except the situation: no variables, state, rendered text, file contents or file names (a file's type, size and hash are kept) Review of answers, labels, statistics. Rerun and replay do not: 409 input_unavailable.  none Nothing The response still has an id", "k": "answers_only full id metadata none", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#opting-out", "h": "Opting out", "x": "Four switches set the level. The most private one always wins: a caller can store less than a template allows, never more.  store in the request body Any decision route true / \"full\", \"answers_only\", false / \"none\". X-Basal-Store header Any decision route; for clients that cannot change the body 1, answers_only, 0. The template's storage Every decision made with that template full, answers_only, n", "k": "answers_only background false full history.store metadata none storage store store_downgraded true X-Basal-Store x-basal-warning", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#sensitive-variables", "h": "Sensitive variables", "x": "Mark a template variable \"sensitive\": true and its value is used for the decision but never written to disk. History keeps a keyed hash in its place (an HMAC, keyed with a secret created for your installation), shows [redacted:<name>] wherever the value appeared, and leaves it out of search.  Because the hash is keyed, nobody can read the value back from it, but you can still find every decision t", "k": "answers_only audio image sensitive_hash store_media video", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#retention", "h": "Retention", "x": "Decisions are deleted automatically once they are older than the retention period: 30 days unless you change it. A template's own retention_days overrides the studio's for its decisions, and 0 means keep forever. Changes apply to decisions already stored, from the next clean-up.  Some decisions are kept regardless of age:  pinned decisions;  decisions you have labelled, while the studio's keep_lab", "k": "keep_labelled retention_days store_media", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#finding-and-exporting", "h": "Finding and exporting", "x": "History is the decisions list, GET /v1/studio/decisions, with filters. You can combine any of them:  template support-triage, support-triage@3 One template, or one version of it. model laya One model's decisions. act false Decisions that needed a person. needs_review urgency Decisions where that answer did not act. answer.<key> answer.department=billing Decisions with that answer. metadata.<key> m", "k": "act correct created_after created_before false invoice labelled laya model needs_review request_id support-triage template true urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#redacting-and-erasing", "h": "Redacting and erasing", "x": "You can remove parts of a stored decision and keep its answers, or delete decisions outright:  POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/redact with fields such as input.variables.message, input.state, input.media or metadata.<key> removes those parts of one decision. Its answers, labels and statistics stay.  POST /v1/studio/decisions/redact and POST /v1/studio/decisions/delete do the same for every decision", "k": "fields filter input.media input.state input.variables.message", "f": ""}, {"t": "History and privacy", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/history.html#where-it-lives-and-who-can-read-it", "h": "Where it lives, and who can read it", "x": "On this computer only. The studio listens on 127.0.0.1 by default. Other websites open in your browser cannot send it requests or read its history.  From other machines, only with a key. When you start the studio with --host 0.0.0.0, set BASAL_API_KEY; clients send Authorization: Bearer <key>. Without a key, remote callers can still make decisions but cannot read History or change anything.  Files", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY blobs fail history_write_failed on_store_error", "f": ""}, {"t": "Fine-tuning and its safeguards", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/fine-tuning.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Fine-tuning and its safeguards", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/fine-tuning.html#what-training-changes", "h": "What training changes", "x": "A decision model reads a situation and the allowed answers, and returns how likely each answer is. Fine-tuning continues the model's training on new examples so that its answers move towards yours.  The studio uses LoRA (low-rank adaptation): the released model's weights stay exactly as they are, and small extra matrices are trained beside some of its layers. The result is a file of changes of 14 ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Fine-tuning and its safeguards", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/fine-tuning.html#how-a-training-run-works", "h": "How a training run works", "x": "Split. Your examples are divided into a part to learn from, a part to check progress on, and a part set aside to test the result (roughly 60/20/20 for small files, 70/15/15 for larger ones). Examples that share a group, or are copies of each other, stay on the same side.  Measure the original. The released model answers the test examples and a fixed set of general questions first (about a thousand", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Fine-tuning and its safeguards", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/fine-tuning.html#the-checks", "h": "The checks", "x": "The new model is added to the studio only if every check passes:  Better on your task it is right more often on the test examples, and a resampling test says that is unlikely to be luck (or it is equally right and clearly better calibrated). Nothing forgotten on the general questions, answered before and after, there is no clear drop of 2 points or more, no drop of 3 points at all, and no clear dr", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Fine-tuning and its safeguards", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/fine-tuning.html#every-model-measured", "h": "Every model, measured", "x": "Each of the eleven models was trained by the studio's own trainer on an NVIDIA GB10 that other programs were using at the same time, with the settings the studio uses by default. Before → after is accuracy on examples set aside before training; General is the change on the general questions, in points. Peak memory is the most GPU memory the training held (\"about\": observed while running rather tha", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Fine-tuning and its safeguards", "s": "Concepts", "u": "concepts/fine-tuning.html#hardware", "h": "Hardware", "x": "NVIDIA RTX 30 series or newer, data-centre GPUs, DGX Spark and other GB10 machines on. Apple M2 or newer, macOS 14 or newer on. Apple M1, or a Mac with less than 12 GB experimental: slower, full precision. NVIDIA RTX 20 series and T4 experimental. AMD GPUs on Linux experimental. Intel Arc, Core Ultra 200V and 200H and newer experimental. processor only, older GPUs, Core Ultra Series 1 graphics, NP", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#before-you-start", "h": "Before you start", "x": "You need the studio open with a model loaded. This guide uses Laya, a small English model that loads in seconds and answers support questions well; any text model works the same way. If you have not installed the studio yet, start with Installation.  The finished template is called support-triage. It takes two variables, the customer's message and their plan, and asks three questions:  Which depar", "k": "support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#try-the-questions-in-the-playground", "h": "Try the questions in the Playground", "x": "The Playground opens on a support ticket example, with these three questions already written. That is the quickest start: adjust them to your own teams and levels, and press Decide to see how the model answers a real ticket.  Open the Playground from the sidebar. The model button at the top left shows the loaded model; press Ctrl L to choose another.  Check the situation. The State box holds the t", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#turn-the-specifics-into-variables", "h": "Turn the specifics into variables", "x": "A template keeps the questions fixed and lets callers fill in the details. Replace the parts of the situation that change from ticket to ticket with {{placeholders}}; each placeholder becomes a variable.  Switch the State box to JSON with the toggle above it, so the plan and the message stay separate fields.  Write the shape of the situation with placeholders, as in the block below this list.  Pre", "k": "account_tier customer_message support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#run-it-in-the-app", "h": "Run it in the app", "x": "After saving, the Playground switches to the template: the left side becomes a form with one field per variable, and the template's questions are locked, so every decision asks exactly the same thing. Fill in a message and a plan, and press Decide.  Below the locked questions you can still add questions of your own for one decision; they are asked alongside the template's and never saved to it. To", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#call-it-from-your-code", "h": "Call it from your code", "x": "Every call sends the template id and the variables, and gets back the answers. The response is the stored decision: its id finds it in History, and the response headers repeat it as x-basal-decision-id.  metadata is optional. It is stored with the decision and never sent to the model, so it is the place for your own ids: here, the ticket number.  The example uses the studio's default address, 127.", "k": "id metadata x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#act-on-confident-answers", "h": "Act on confident answers", "x": "Each answer has act: true when its certainty reached the template's act threshold, 0.9 here. The decision's own act is true only when every answer acts, and needs_review lists the ones that did not.  In the response above the model leans the right way on every question (technical, blocking, no churn threat), but none of the three answers is 90% sure, so the whole ticket goes to a person. That is t", "k": "act id needs_review settings true", "f": "act decision id needs_review"}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#handle-mistakes", "h": "Handle mistakes", "x": "A request the template cannot accept is refused before any model runs, with every problem listed at once. A misspelled variable name is reported with the name you probably meant, and the missing variable is reported beside it.  These errors are mistakes in the request, so retrying will not help; fix the request. Call the studio from your code lists which errors are worth retrying", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#give-a-variable-a-type", "h": "Give a variable a type", "x": "The Playground saves every placeholder as free text. account_tier should only ever be one of three plans, so give it a list of allowed values and a default. The change is a PATCH with only the fields that change, and it saves version 2; version 1 stays exactly as it was.  enum The allowed values. Anything else is refused with invalid_variable.  default Used when a caller leaves the variable out.  ", "k": "account_tier default enum invalid_variable note PATCH", "f": "default enum note"}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#review-the-decisions-in-history", "h": "Review the decisions in History", "x": "Every call above is in History, with the ticket's message, the answers and the template version. Filter by the template to see its decisions on their own; the ones that asked a person are marked Ask a human. From a decision you can label the right answers, rerun it on another model, or open it in the Playground.  Fig. 5 History filtered to Support triage. The decisions from curl, Python and Node.j", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Build a support-triage template", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/support-triage.html#next-steps", "h": "Next steps", "x": "Improve a template safely: label decisions, turn them into test examples, save a better version and promote it with an alias.  Templates and versions: every field a template holds, and how versions, aliases and extensions work.  Call the studio from your code: retries, idempotency keys and slow model starts", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#the-address-and-keys", "h": "The address and keys", "x": "While the app is open, the studio listens at http://127.0.0.1:8420. If that port is taken, the desktop app picks the first free port from 8421 to 8440 and remembers it; the API page in the app always shows the exact address.  Calls from the same computer need no key. To call the studio from other machines, start it with --host 0.0.0.0 and set the environment variable BASAL_API_KEY; clients then se", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY Content-Type", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#a-small-client", "h": "A small client", "x": "The client on the right does four things:  sends one decision and returns the stored decision object;  raises an error that carries the studio's code, message and param, so your code can branch on the code;  retries only what is worth retrying, with a pause that grows each time;  sends the same idempotency key on every attempt, so a retry can never make a second decision.  It needs no SDK: httpx i", "k": "code fetch httpx message param", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#errors", "h": "Errors", "x": "Every error from /v1/studio has the same envelope: a type, a machine-readable code, a message written for people, the param that caused it (such as variables.account_tier ), and details listing every problem found in the same pass. When a failed decision was still stored, for example because the model crashed, decision_id points at it.  What to do depends on the status:  400 The request is wrong: ", "k": "code decision_id details idempotency_in_progress message param retry-after type variables.account_tier", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#retries-and-idempotency-keys", "h": "Retries and idempotency keys", "x": "Networks drop responses. If your client times out after the studio has made a decision, a plain retry makes a second decision, and History counts the ticket twice. An idempotency key prevents that: send the header Idempotency-Key with a value unique to the logical call (a UUID, or a stable id such as ticket-T-5130-triage ), and send the same value on every retry of that call.  Same key, same body,", "k": "Idempotency-Key ticket-T-5130-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#slow-starts-background-and-waiting", "h": "Slow starts: background and waiting", "x": "Most decisions take milliseconds, but the first call to a model that is not loaded yet loads it first. That takes from a few seconds for a small model to several minutes for a large one, and the request waits up to 15 minutes. A client with a short timeout gives up before the answer arrives.  There are two ways to cope:  Load models ahead of time, from the app or from your deployment script, and g", "k": "completed failed id interrupted Location status", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#choosing-what-history-keeps", "h": "Choosing what History keeps", "x": "Every call is recorded in History by default. Three request options control what is kept:  \"store\": false Nothing is written. The response still has an id for your logs.  \"store\": \"answers_only\" The answers, settings and metadata are kept; the situation is not.  metadata Up to 16 keys of your own, stored with the decision and never sent to the model. Use ids, such as a ticket number, and filter Hi", "k": "id metadata sensitive", "f": "\"store\": \"answers_only\" \"store\": false metadata"}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#checking-a-request-without-running-it", "h": "Checking a request without running it", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/preview takes the same body as a decision and runs every check: the template version it resolves to, the variables, the rendered situation, the effective questions, the model and the settings with their sources. It runs no model and stores nothing. Use it in tests and in CI to catch a broken template or a renamed variable before production does", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Call the studio from your code", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/from-code.html#where-to-go-next", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Decisions: every field and option of a decision.  Templates: create and version templates from a script.  Let an agent choose its next action: a decision inside an agent loop", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#the-starting-point", "h": "The starting point", "x": "This guide continues the support-triage template from the triage guide. It is at version 2, the production alias points at version 2, and your code calls support-triage@production. A month of tickets has gone through it, and reviewers have labelled the right answers on some of them in History.  Promoting through an alias is what makes the rest of this guide safe. A new version is used straight awa", "k": "production support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#find-the-weak-question", "h": "Find the weak question", "x": "The template's statistics break down every question by version: how often it acts on its own, how certain it is on average and, where decisions were labelled, how often it was right. In the app this is the template's History tab; over the API it is the template's stats.  In the response on the right (shortened to version 2), urgency never acts on its own, its average certainty is 49%, and it was r", "k": "stats urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#turn-labelled-decisions-into-test-examples", "h": "Turn labelled decisions into test examples", "x": "A test example is an input with its right answers, kept with the template. Examples outlive History's retention, so they are the fixed set you check every new version against.  The quickest way to build the set is from decisions reviewers have already labelled. In the app, open a labelled decision in History, then More, then Add to support-triage's test examples. Over the API, post the decision's ", "k": "examples_revision from_decision", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#save-the-new-version", "h": "Save the new version", "x": "The fix is in the wording: describe what each urgency level means, so the model has something to tell \"today\" from \"blocking\" by. Wording-only changes keep every question comparable with version 2, so the old and new answers can be charted side by side.  Send only what changes with PATCH, and add If-Match with the version you started from. If someone saved another version in the meantime, the save", "k": "If-Match PATCH production", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#what-changed", "h": "What changed", "x": "Every version records how it differs from the one before. Here the class is wording: only text changed, callers keep working ( breaking_for_callers is false ), and urgency is text_changed while the other questions are identical. Templates and versions explains each class.  The same PATCH sent again with If-Match: \"2\" now fails, because the template has moved on to version 3", "k": "breaking_for_callers false identical PATCH text_changed urgency wording", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#run-the-examples-on-both-versions", "h": "Run the examples on both versions", "x": "Run every test example on version 2 and on version 3, and count how often each question gets the labelled answer. The script on the right does that in a dozen lines; metadata tags each decision with its example, so the runs are easy to find in History.  On these six examples, version 3 gets urgency right four times instead of two, and the other questions are unchanged. Six examples are a small sam", "k": "metadata urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#compare-the-versions-on-the-same-inputs", "h": "Compare the versions on the same inputs", "x": "The template's Compare versions tab, and GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/compare?versions=2,3, compare two versions over History: how answers are distributed, how often each acts, how certain it is and, for decisions with labels, how accurate it is.  Because the script ran both versions on identical variables, the comparison can also pair them: Same input, same answer is the share of pairs where the", "k": "urgency", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#promote-with-an-alias", "h": "Promote with an alias", "x": "Point production at version 3. Every caller of support-triage@production uses version 3 from the next request, with no deploy. The response includes previous_version, so you know where the way back is.  In the app, open the template's Versions tab and choose Set alias on version 3.  Fig. 3 The Versions tab after promoting: version 3 is both latest and production, with its note and the change summa", "k": "latest previous_version production", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#roll-back", "h": "Roll back", "x": "If version 3 misbehaves in production, move the alias back. It takes effect on the next request, and nothing about version 3 is lost.  Callers of the bare id follow latest, not an alias. To return them to the old behaviour as well, restore version 2: POST /v1/studio/templates/support-triage/versions/2/restore saves its definition as a new version 4 (the response says \"change\": \"new_version\" ), so ", "k": "latest", "f": ""}, {"t": "Improve a template safely", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/improve.html#a-routine-that-keeps-working", "h": "A routine that keeps working", "x": "Label decisions that asked a person; that is where the model is weakest and where labels teach the most.  Promote labelled decisions to test examples, and keep the set growing.  Change one thing per version, and write a note that says why.  Run the examples on the new version and the current production version before moving the alias.  Watch the comparison on real traffic for a few days after prom", "k": "note", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#prepare-your-examples", "h": "Prepare your examples", "x": "A training file is a list of past situations with the answer you would give. The simplest is a spreadsheet saved as CSV: one column with the text, and one column per answer.  Fig. 1 What should the file look like? on the Train page shows the same shape. From this file the studio builds two questions: team, a Pick one question with the team names it found, and urgent, a Yes or no question asked as ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#the-standard-format", "h": "The standard format", "x": "For questions a table can't express (scales with named levels, pick-all-that-apply, option descriptions, or different questions on different rows) use JSON lines: one example per line, written as a decision request plus the right answers.  state required the situation: text, or an object or list. questions required the question definitions a decision request takes. answers required the right answe", "k": "answers calibration choice false gold group id label media multi noul number questions rank score split state test text train true typed-decisions", "f": "answers group id media questions split state"}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#train", "h": "Train", "x": "Open Train and add the file. Drop it on the page or choose Choose a file. The page lists the questions it found and any problems.  Check the questions. Reword each instruction the way you would ask it; the model will be asked it in exactly those words later.  Check the model and the time. The recommendation suits most tasks. Choose Change model for another; models that can't learn these examples s", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#read-the-result", "h": "Read the result", "x": "The result page gives two numbers. Correct on questions it had never seen is the one that matters: the studio set those examples aside before training and never trained on them. General decisions it already knew is the check that nothing was forgotten. One held-out example the original got wrong is shown with both answers.  Use it now opens that example in the Playground with the new model.  Compa", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#when-the-result-isnt-kept", "h": "When the result isn't kept", "x": "The page names the reason. What usually helps:  Didn't beat the original model more examples, especially of rare answers; clearer instructions or option descriptions; or Teach another model instead. Got worse at general decisions more varied examples; the studio has already retried once more gently. The same answer to almost everything one answer dominates your file; add examples of the others. Th", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#use-it-from-code", "h": "Use it from code", "x": "A trained model has its own id, shown in the Playground's Code tab. Call it like any other model, or make it a template's default model.  Ask it the questions it was trained on, in the same words: that is where it improved. It still answers anything the original model could", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Teach a model your own decisions", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/teach.html#move-it-to-another-computer", "h": "Move it to another computer", "x": "On the Train page, Export on the model's row saves it as one .zip: the small file of changes and a record of how it was trained, not the whole model. On the other computer, choose Import a trained model on its Train page. The model it was trained from downloads there like any other model; the studio says if it needs it", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#the-idea", "h": "The idea", "x": "Each step of an agent loop has the same shape: here is the goal, here is what happened so far, here is what the last action returned; which action now? That is a Pick one question whose options are the actions available at this step.  Two things make a decision model a good fit:  It cannot invent an action. The answer is always one of the options you sent, so there is no output to parse and no too", "k": "options", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#the-template", "h": "The template", "x": "The template on the right has four variables:  goal string What the agent is trying to do.  steps json What it has done so far, oldest first.  observation string What the last action returned.  actions options The actions available now, each with a short description.  \"criteria\": \"{{actions}}\" makes next_action take its options from the actions variable on every call. A second question, goal_reach", "k": "actions goal goal_reached json next_action observation options PUT steps string", "f": "actions goal observation steps"}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#one-step", "h": "One step", "x": "Each step sends the goal, the steps so far, the last observation and the actions on offer. Here an agent investigating slow checkouts has found that release v2.14 changed a retry policy, and has four actions plus \"finish\" to choose from.  The model puts 77% on reading the code change, above the 0.7 threshold, so next_action.act is true and the agent may go ahead. The answer carries dynamic_options", "k": "next_action.act true", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#the-loop", "h": "The loop", "x": "The loop below runs until the agent finishes, escalates, or meets an answer it is not sure enough about. It reads answers.next_action.act, not the decision's overall act: the agent only needs to be sure of its next action, and goal_reached is there as a second opinion, not a gate.  CAREFUL raises the bar for actions that change production. A rollback needs 95% certainty, whatever the template's th", "k": "act answers.next_action.act CAREFUL goal_reached", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#what-the-model-read", "h": "What the model read", "x": "The variables become one situation, with field names as labels. Check this text in History (the decision's Input tab) or with include=input.rendered_state, especially when an agent behaves unexpectedly: the model only knows what is written here.  Keep each step short and factual. The context of the model is limited (8,192 tokens for Intern-Decision 4B), so a long run should summarise older steps r", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#practical-rules", "h": "Practical rules", "x": "Name actions as your code does. The option name is what comes back, so use your tool names ( read_diff, not \"Read the diff\"), and put the explanation in the description.  Offer only what is possible now. If a tool is unavailable at this step, leave it out of actions. The model can only choose among what you send.  Always offer a way out. An ask_human or finish option gives the model somewhere to p", "k": "actions ask_human CAREFUL finish metadata.run read_diff", "f": ""}, {"t": "Let an agent choose its next action", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/agents.html#review-a-run-in-history", "h": "Review a run in History", "x": "Every step is a stored decision. Filter History, or the API, by the run's metadata to see each step in order with its probabilities. Label the steps where the agent chose badly, then promote them to test examples, as Improve a template safely describes, so the next version of the template, or the next model you try, is measured on exactly those situations", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#which-models-read-what", "h": "Which models read what", "x": "Intern-Decision 4B yes no no 10 GB of memory; runs on the GPU or the processor. Jev-Omni yes yes yes 26 GB of memory; needs an NVIDIA GPU. Every other model no no no Text only. A request with media for a model that cannot read it is refused before anything runs, with 400 model_incompatible and a modality_not_supported detail. The Images, audio and video examples in the Playground (a receipt photo ", "k": "modality_not_supported", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#upload-a-file", "h": "Upload a file", "x": "Upload the file once, then refer to it by its id. POST /v1/studio/files takes a multipart form field named file, or the raw bytes with the file name in the X-Filename header.  The response names the file file_…, its kind, size and a SHA-256 hash of its content. An uploaded file that no decision or test example uses is removed after 24 hours ( expires_at ); once a decision uses it, it is kept as lo", "k": "expires_at file X-Filename", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#ask-about-it", "h": "Ask about it", "x": "Attach the file in media as {\"type\": \"image\", \"file_id\": \"file_…\"}, next to a short situation that says what the image is for. The questions are ordinary questions.  The photo here is the sample receipt from the Playground's Check a receipt photo example. Intern-Decision 4B recognises it as a till receipt (98%) and finds the olive oil on it (93%). The legibility rating, 84% \"readable\", is below th", "k": "media", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#send-it-inline", "h": "Send it inline", "x": "For a one-off decision you can skip the upload and put the file in the request as a data: URL: \"data\": \"data:image/png;base64,…\". Inline files work on every route, including TypeSafe's /v1/systemone, which has no file upload of its own.  The response from /v1/systemone is TypeSafe's plain shape, and the decision is stored in History all the same; the x-basal-decision-id header names it", "k": "x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#in-a-template", "h": "In a template", "x": "A template takes a file through a variable of type image, audio or video, and lists that kind in modalities. Callers send the file id (or a data: URL) as the variable's value; the file is attached in the order the variables are declared, before any media in the request. A media variable can never appear inside text.  In the app, a template with a media variable shows a drop zone for it in the Play", "k": "audio image media modalities video", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#audio-and-video", "h": "Audio and video", "x": "Audio and video work the same way, with \"type\": \"audio\" or \"type\": \"video\" and Jev-Omni as the model. Jev-Omni needs an NVIDIA GPU with about 26 GB of free memory; load it from the Models page first, since loading takes a while.  A recorded call \"media\": [{\"type\": \"audio\", \"file_id\": \"file_…\"}] with questions such as \"Does the caller ask to cancel?\". A short clip \"media\": [{\"type\": \"video\", \"file_", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decide about images, audio and video", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/media.html#what-history-keeps-of-a-file", "h": "What History keeps of a file", "x": "Files are stored once by their content, so the same photo sent twenty times takes the space of one.  store: \"full\" (the default) Kept with the decision, shown in History, and reusable for a rerun. store: \"answers_only\" Not kept. History records its kind, size and hash, but not its name or content. store: false Nothing is kept; an inline file is deleted as soon as the model has read it. store_media", "k": "audio image sensitive store_media video", "f": "store: \"answers_only\" store: \"full\" store: false store_media"}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#change-the-base-url", "h": "Change the base URL", "x": "Point your client at the studio and keep everything else. With the official SDKs, set base_url (Python) or baseURL (JavaScript) to the studio's address, and use any API key: the studio does not check keys from this computer.  The model name can be any studio model id, such as laya, or TypeSafe's own names: jev-latest and the other Jev names mean the most recently loaded model.  Both SDK examples o", "k": "base_url baseURL jev-latest laya typesafe-sdk", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#what-stays-the-same", "h": "What stays the same", "x": "On /v1/systemone the response body is exactly TypeSafe's: the same envelope ( model, answers, usage ), the same fields on each answer, the same request id header and the same validation errors ( 422 with a detail list). The studio's test suite checks this against TypeSafe's published schema and both official SDKs.  Request body model, state, questions The same; also media, settings.temperature and", "k": "answers choice confidence detail jev-latest legend media model multi noul number probabilities questions rank score settings.temperature state type usage x-basal-decision-id x-basal-stored x-typesafe-request-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#more-detail-when-you-want-it", "h": "More detail when you want it", "x": "Send the header X-Basal-Extensions: 1 and each answer also carries the studio's fields: decision, top_probability and the full probabilities, plus latency_ms, passes and notes on the response. Some models add signals of their own under model_extras; Laya, for instance, reports its built-in act gate. The official SDKs keep the fields they know and ignore the rest.  The studio's own API returns all ", "k": "act certainty decision latency_ms model_extras notes passes probabilities top_probability", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#your-calls-are-in-history", "h": "Your calls are in History", "x": "Every call to /v1/systemone is recorded in History with its situation, questions and full answers, kept for 30 days by default. That happens without any change to your code; the record is a full studio decision, with certainty and an act gate at the studio's default threshold, even though your response was TypeSafe's plain shape.  Find a call by the decision id from the x-basal-decision-id header,", "k": "certainty source x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#keeping-calls-out-of-history", "h": "Keeping calls out of History", "x": "To keep nothing for a call, add \"store\": false to the body, or send the header X-Basal-Store: 0 if you cannot change the body. \"store\": \"answers_only\" (or X-Basal-Store: answers_only) keeps the answers without the situation. With the official Python SDK, pass extra_body={\"store\": False}.  TypeSafe's servers ignore these fields, so the same code runs against both. The studio reads them leniently: a", "k": "x-basal-warning", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#gateway-formats", "h": "Gateway formats", "x": "The studio also serves the formats of the two gateways that offer TypeSafe's models. Each route returns exactly that gateway's response and error shapes, so their clients work unchanged.  POST /v1/systemone, GET /v1/models TypeSafe's Jev API. POST /api/v1/systemone, POST /api/alpha/decisions OpenRouter's Decisions API: adds id, provider and usage.cost. POST /typesafe/v1/systemone, GET /typesafe/v1", "k": "boolean id probability provider provider_metadata usage.cost", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#label-calls-with-a-template", "h": "Label calls with a template", "x": "If your code sends the same questions every time, save them as a template and label each call with the header X-Basal-Template: <id>@<version>. The response does not change, but History attributes the call to that template version, so it appears in the template's history, statistics and version comparisons.  The call is attributed only when every question of that version is in the request with an ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#move-to-the-studio-api-step-by-step", "h": "Move to the studio API step by step", "x": "Each step is optional, and each keeps working on its own.  Change nothing. Your calls are already in History, and you can review, label and export them.  Change the path, keep the body. Any valid /v1/systemone body is also a valid POST /v1/studio/decisions body. Answers keep their keys and their TypeSafe fields, and gain decision, certainty, act and origin, plus id, act and needs_review on the dec", "k": "act certainty decision id needs_review origin production state variables X-Basal-Extensions", "f": ""}, {"t": "Move from Jev or a gateway", "s": "Guides", "u": "guides/migrate.html#differences-to-know", "h": "Differences to know", "x": "The model must be on this computer. A model that is downloaded but not loaded is loaded by the first call, which can take minutes; a model that is not downloaded returns an error. TypeSafe's SDK times out after 10 seconds by default, so load models ahead of time or raise the timeout.  jev-latest is whichever model you loaded last. Name a model explicitly when the choice matters.  Calls are recorde", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY jev-latest", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html", "h": "", "x": "The studio runs a small web server while the app is open (or while python -m basal.server runs from source). Its API takes JSON and returns JSON. The examples on these pages were captured from a running studio with the Laya model loaded; only ids and timings differ from what you will see.  Code examples use three forms. curl runs in any terminal. Python uses httpx ( pip install httpx ). JavaScript uses the fetch built into Node.js 18 and later, written as an ES module so await works at the top level", "k": "await fetch", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#base-url", "h": "Base URL", "x": "The studio listens on http://127.0.0.1:8420. The desktop app prefers port 8420; when another program holds it, the app takes the first free port from 8421 to 8440 and keeps using it. The API page in the app always shows the address in use.  A quick way to check the studio is up is to ask for a model:  id string The model id you send in model, such as laya.  status string loaded, loading, downloade", "k": "context_tokens downloaded id laya loaded loading model not_downloaded status", "f": "context_tokens id status"}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#three-families-of-endpoints", "h": "Three families of endpoints", "x": "The studio serves three groups of endpoints. They share one engine, so a decision made through any of them uses the same models and lands in the same history.  The studio API /v1/studio/... New code. Templates, decisions with history, feedback, test examples, files and settings. Errors come in one envelope.  TypeSafe and gateway formats /v1/systemone, /v1/models, /api/v1/systemone, /api/alpha/deci", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#authentication-and-remote-access", "h": "Authentication and remote access", "x": "By default the studio listens on this computer only and needs no key. Requests from other programs on the same computer, such as your scripts, are accepted as they are.  To serve other machines, start the studio with --host 0.0.0.0 (or BASAL_HOST=0.0.0.0) and set BASAL_API_KEY. Remote callers then send Authorization: Bearer <key>. Callers on the studio's own computer still need no key unless BASAL", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY http preview", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#calling-from-a-web-page", "h": "Calling from a web page", "x": "Every decision is recorded, so the studio refuses writes that come from other websites open in your browser. A request that carries an Origin header must come from the studio's own address or from an origin listed in BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS (comma-separated). Listing an origin also turns on CORS for it.  Browser writes to /v1/studio must also send Content-Type: application/json. Requests without browse", "k": "BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS Origin", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#headers", "h": "Headers", "x": "Headers the studio reads:  Authorization All Bearer <BASAL_API_KEY>, when the studio requires a key.  Idempotency-Key Any POST that makes a decision Makes a retry safe: the same key and body return the first response instead of deciding again. See Retries and idempotency.  X-Basal-Store Decisions full, answers_only or none (also 1 and 0 ). Lowers what history keeps for this call.  X-Basal-Metadata", "k": "answers_only attributed Authorization etag failed false full Idempotency-Key If-Match location metadata mismatch none POST req_ retry-after server-timing source.request_id true x-basal-decision-id X-Basal-Extensions X-Basal-Metadata X-Basal-Store x-basal-stored X-Basal-Template x-basal-template-status x-basal-version-created x-basal-warning x-request-id X-TypeSafe-Request-Id x-typesafe-request-id X-TypeSafe-Retry-Count", "f": "Authorization etag Idempotency-Key If-Match location retry-after server-timing x-basal-decision-id X-Basal-Extensions x-basal-idempotent-replayed X-Basal-Metadata X-Basal-Store x-basal-stored X-Basal-Template x-basal-version-created x-basal-warning x-request-id X-TypeSafe-Request-Id X-TypeSafe-Retry-Count"}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#objects-ids-and-times", "h": "Objects, ids and times", "x": "Objects carry an object field naming their kind: decision, decision.summary, template, template.version, feedback, file, list and so on. Ids start with a prefix that names the kind: dec_ for decisions, fb_ for feedback, ex_ for test examples and file_ for files. The rest of an id is a ULID, so ids sort in the order they were made. Templates use the id you choose, such as support-triage; starter te", "k": "completed_at created_at dec_ decision decision.summary ex_ expires_at fb_ feedback file file_ list object support-triage template template.version", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#pages-of-results", "h": "Pages of results", "x": "Lists return at most limit items (1 to 100, default 20), newest first. To read the next page, pass the previous page's last_id as after. has_more says whether another page exists. Pass order=asc to read oldest first.  data array The items on this page.  first_id string The id of the first item, or null for an empty page.  last_id string The id of the last item. Send it as after for the next page. ", "k": "after data first_id has_more last_id limit null true", "f": "data first_id has_more last_id"}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#slow-first-calls-and-background-decisions", "h": "Slow first calls and background decisions", "x": "A decision normally takes a fraction of a second. The first decision for a model that is downloaded but not loaded takes longer: the studio loads the model first, which can take from a few seconds to a few minutes for the largest models. Set your client's timeout accordingly (the examples use 120 seconds), or load the model in the app beforehand.  For clients with short timeouts, send \"background\"", "k": "location", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#retries-and-idempotency", "h": "Retries and idempotency", "x": "Send an Idempotency-Key header, any string of up to 255 printable characters, with each logical call. If the network fails and you retry with the same key and the same body, the studio returns the saved response with x-basal-idempotent-replayed: true instead of running the decision twice. Keys are kept for 24 hours.  Same key and body, first request succeeded The first response is replayed, with i", "k": "Idempotency-Key source.retry_of X-TypeSafe-Retry-Count", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#errors", "h": "Errors", "x": "Every error from /v1/studio comes in one envelope: an error object with the fields type, code, message, param, details, request_id and decision_id. code is a stable name to branch on, param points at the field that caused it, and details lists every problem found in the same pass, so one round trip shows all of them. The wire formats keep their own error shapes. See Errors for every code", "k": "code decision_id details error message param request_id type", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#interactive-reference", "h": "Interactive reference", "x": "The studio also serves an interactive reference generated from the code, at http://127.0.0.1:8420/docs, with the raw OpenAPI document at /openapi.json. Every studio API operation there has ready-to-run examples. Requests sent from that page are real: they run models and are stored in history like any other", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "API reference", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/index.html#every-endpoint", "h": "Every endpoint", "x": "Decisions and history:  POST /v1/studio/decisions Create a decision. POST /v1/studio/decisions/preview Preview a decision without running a model. GET /v1/studio/decisions/{id} Retrieve a decision. GET /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/input Retrieve a decision's input. POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/rerun Rerun a decision. POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/cancel Cancel a background decision. PATCH /v1/stud", "k": "DELETE GET PATCH POST PUT", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html", "h": "", "x": "All decision endpoints live under /v1/studio/decisions. The examples use a template named support-triage, which routes a customer message to a department, rates its urgency on a four-level scale and asks whether the customer threatens to leave. Templates shows how it was created", "k": "support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#create-a-decision", "h": "Create a decision", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions Runs a model and returns its answers. Send a template and its variables to reuse saved questions, or send state and questions for a decision without a template. The response comes back when the model has answered, usually in a fraction of a second; a model that still has to load takes longer (see slow first calls ).  Each answer carries a certainty and an act flag. An ans", "k": "act act_threshold add_options answers.model_extras background certainty false Idempotency-Key include input input.rendered_state laya location media metadata model needs_review questions settings settings.sources skip state store support-triage temperature template true unknown_field_ignored variables warnings x-basal-decision-id X-Basal-Metadata X-Basal-Store", "f": "add_options background include media metadata model questions settings skip state store template variables"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#without-a-template", "h": "Without a template", "x": "Send state and questions directly. The body of a TypeSafe /v1/systemone request works here as it is, and gains history, metadata and the act gate. Without a template, the act threshold comes from settings, or else from the studio's default (0.9 unless you change it in settings ).  The response above is shortened to its main fields; the full object has every field shown in Create a decision", "k": "questions settings state", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#extend-a-template-for-one-call", "h": "Extend a template for one call", "x": "A template decides which of its questions callers may change. Its extensions setting can allow extra questions (up to max_questions ), new options for named choice, multi, rank and number questions, and skipping named questions. Options can never be added to score or noul questions, because a new level would change what the answer means. Unless the template says otherwise, it accepts up to 16 extr", "k": "add_options adhoc choice confidence extended extensions extra legend max_questions multi noul number origin questions rank score skip template top_probability", "f": "add_options questions skip"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#the-decision-object", "h": "The decision object", "x": "id string dec_ followed by a time-ordered id.  status string completed, failed, or for background decisions queued, in_progress and cancelled.  created_at, completed_at integer Unix seconds.  template object id, version, the ref you sent, resolved_from ( latest, pinned or alias) and attribution ( explicit; header for a wire-format call filed under a template; draft for the Playground's unsaved edi", "k": "act adhoc alias answers answers_only api attempt attribution batch cancelled certainty choice client compare completed completed_at confidence created_at dec_ decision draft endpoint error estimate eval expires_at explicit extended extensions extra failed feedback format full group header id in_progress include input input_tokens latest legend load_ms media metadata model model_ms model_requested model_revision most_likely multi needs_review no none notes noul null number origin output_tokens passes pinned playground probabilities questions queue_ms queued range rank ranking raw_probabilities ref request_id rerun rerun_of resolved_from retry_of score selected settings source sources state status store surface temperature template threshold timing top_probability total_ms true type unit usage variables version warnings yes", "f": "act answers batch certainty created_at decision error expires_at extensions feedback group id input metadata model model_revision needs_review origin passes probabilities settings source status store template timing top_probability type usage warnings"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#preview-a-decision", "h": "Preview a decision", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/preview Checks a request and shows exactly what the model would receive, without running it or storing anything. It takes the same body as Create a decision. Use it to check variables, see the rendered situation, or turn a template call into the equivalent /v1/systemone request.  state, rendered_state The situation with the variables filled in, and the text the model read", "k": "questions rendered_state settings state state_may_be_truncated store systemone_request warnings worker_settings", "f": "questions settings state store systemone_request warnings worker_settings"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#run-a-decision-in-the-background", "h": "Run a decision in the background", "x": "Add \"background\": true to a create request. The studio stores the decision with status: \"queued\", answers at once, and runs it. Collect the result with Retrieve a decision and ?wait=60, which holds the request open for up to 60 seconds and returns as soon as the decision has finished.  A background decision must be stored so it can be fetched, so store: false is refused with background_requires_st", "k": "failed", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#retrieve-a-decision", "h": "Retrieve a decision", "x": "GET /v1/studio/decisions/{id} Returns a stored decision, including its input. A decision made with store: \"none\" was never saved and answers 404 decision_not_found, as does one removed by retention.  wait Up to 60 seconds to wait for a queued or running decision to finish.  include input.rendered_state adds the text the model read; answers.raw_probabilities adds the probabilities before calibratio", "k": "answers evaluate format include input input.rendered_state openrouter typesafe vercel wait", "f": "format include wait"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#in-another-apis-format", "h": "In another API's format", "x": "?format=typesafe returns a stored decision's answers in the exact shape /v1/systemone returns, which helps when code written for TypeSafe reads decisions back from history. openrouter, vercel and evaluate work the same way for the gateway formats; add extended=true for the studio's extra answer fields", "k": "evaluate openrouter vercel", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#retrieve-a-decisions-input", "h": "Retrieve a decision's input", "x": "GET /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/input Returns only what the model was given: the variables, the situation, media, the questions, and the rendered text the model read. It answers 409 input_unavailable when the decision kept only its answers or its input was redacted", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#rerun-a-decision", "h": "Rerun a decision", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/rerun Runs a stored decision's input again, by default on the same model, the same template version and the settings you sent the first time. Change any of them in the body to see how the answer moves. The new decision links back with rerun_of, its source.surface is rerun, and it copies the original's metadata.  model string Another model.  template string Another te", "k": "answers include model rerun rerun_of settings settings.questions source.surface store template variables", "f": "include model settings store template variables"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#cancel-a-background-decision", "h": "Cancel a background decision", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/cancel Cancels a background decision that has not reached the model yet, for example one waiting for its model to load. It returns the decision with status: \"cancelled\". A decision the model is already working on, or one that has finished, answers 409 decision_finished.  If the decision itself started that model loading and no other request is waiting for the model, ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#update-a-decision", "h": "Update a decision", "x": "PATCH /v1/studio/decisions/{id} A decision's answers, input and settings never change once made. You can change two things:  pinned boolean true keeps the decision whatever the retention setting says; expires_at becomes null.  metadata object A merge patch: keys you send are set, keys set to null are removed, other keys stay.  Sending any other field answers 400 read_only_field. The response is th", "k": "expires_at metadata null pinned true", "f": "metadata pinned"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#redact-a-decision", "h": "Redact a decision", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/redact Removes parts of a decision's input and keeps its answers, so statistics stay correct after you delete personal data. Redacted values read [redacted], and the input lists what was removed under redacted.  fields array Required. Any of input.state, input.rendered_state, input.variables, input.variables.<name>, input.media and metadata.<key>.  Removing variables", "k": "fields input.media input.rendered_state input.state input.variables redacted", "f": "fields"}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#delete-a-decision", "h": "Delete a decision", "x": "DELETE /v1/studio/decisions/{id} Deletes a decision with everything stored for it: input, answers, feedback and the link to any media. This cannot be undone. Media files it used are cleaned up later, once nothing else refers to them", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Decisions", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/decisions.html#choose-what-history-keeps", "h": "Choose what history keeps", "x": "Every decision is stored at one of three levels. The level actually used is the most private of what the request asks for, what the template allows (its storage field) and the studio's setting ( history.store ), so a request can keep less than the studio does but never more. When a level was lowered for you, warnings says so with store_downgraded.  Everything ( full) Input, answers, settings, meta", "k": "answers_only full history.store id none sensitive storage store store_downgraded warnings x-basal-stored", "f": ""}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html", "h": "", "x": "History holds decisions from every entry point: the studio API, /v1/systemone and the gateway formats, the Playground and Compare. What each decision keeps depends on its storage level, and old decisions are removed by retention (30 days unless you change it in settings ). Pinned decisions, and by default labelled ones, are never removed by retention", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html#list-decisions", "h": "List decisions", "x": "GET /v1/studio/decisions Returns a page of decision summaries, newest first. A summary carries each answer as one label ( decision) with its certainty, which is enough to scan a list; fetch a single decision for the input and the probabilities, or pass view=full to get full objects in the list.  Filters combine with AND. Most accept several values separated by commas, which combine with OR.  templ", "k": "act after all api asc attribution before cancelled client compare completed correct created_after created_before decision desc draft endpoint explicit extended failed false fold_retries format full header in_progress include input input.rendered_state labelled limit model needs_review none order pinned playground queued request_id rerun rerun_of status summary surface template true version view", "f": "&lt;key&gt; &lt;question&gt; act attribution client correct created_after extended fold_retries include labelled limit model needs_review pinned q rerun_of status surface template version view"}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html#decision-statistics", "h": "Decision statistics", "x": "GET /v1/studio/decisions/stats Summarises every decision that matches the list filters: how many there were, how often they acted, how fast they were, and for each question how answers were spread, how sure the model was and, where you labelled decisions, how often it was right.  group_by Split the summary: version, template, model, model_revision, surface, client, format, status, day or hour. Sev", "k": "accuracy accuracy_when_acting act_rate client count day distribution excluded_runs failed format group_by hour include_extended labelled latency_ms mean_certainty model model_revision null p50 p95 questions runs status surface template true version what_if.act_threshold what_if.temperature", "f": "act_rate act_threshold distribution group_by include_extended labelled mean_certainty questions runs temperature"}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html#export-decisions", "h": "Export decisions", "x": "GET /v1/studio/decisions/export Downloads every decision matching the list filters, as a file.  format jsonl (the default): one decision object per line, without its input unless you ask for it with include. csv: one row per decision with each question's label, certainty and act flag, and one column per metadata key.  wire_format Filters by the API format of the call ( typesafe, studio, ...). Here", "k": "csv format include input input.rendered_state jsonl studio typesafe wire_format", "f": "format include wire_format"}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html#label-a-decision", "h": "Label a decision", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/feedback Records the right answer to some or all of a decision's questions, a thumbs-up or thumbs-down rating, or both. Labels feed accuracy in statistics, can become test examples, and keep the decision safe from retention while keep_labelled is on. Labelling the same question again replaces the earlier label.  expected object Question key to the right answer.  rati", "k": "accuracy choice correct expected false keep_labelled multi note noul number predicted range rank rating score true", "f": "expected note rating"}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html#list-and-delete-feedback", "h": "List and delete feedback", "x": "GET /v1/studio/decisions/{id}/feedback DELETE /v1/studio/feedback/{id} GET returns every feedback entry of a decision, oldest first, in the same list shape as above. DELETE removes one entry by its fb_ id. The decision's feedback field always shows the latest label per question", "k": "DELETE fb_ feedback GET", "f": ""}, {"t": "History, feedback and statistics", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/history.html#delete-or-redact-many-decisions", "h": "Delete or redact many decisions", "x": "POST /v1/studio/decisions/delete POST /v1/studio/decisions/redact Deletes, or removes input from, every decision that matches a filter. The filter takes the same keys as List decisions, written as a JSON object. Run with dry_run: true first: it counts what would change and changes nothing.  filter object List filters, such as {\"template\": \"support-triage\", \"created_before\": \"-30d\"}. Every surface ", "k": "all dry_run fields filter include_pinned sensitive sensitive_hash surface true", "f": "all dry_run fields filter include_pinned"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html", "h": "", "x": "A template has two parts. The definition (variables, state, questions, model, settings, extensions, modalities) is versioned: any change to it saves a new, immutable version. The head (name, description, metadata, storage and retention) is not versioned, and changing it saves no version.  Callers name a template by reference: support-triage runs the latest version, support-triage@2 pins version 2, and support-triage@production follows the alias production, which you move when a new version is ready. Starter templates are read-only and named builtin/<name>; clone one to change it. Templates and", "k": "production support-triage", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#create-a-template", "h": "Create a template", "x": "POST /v1/studio/templates Creates a template at version 1 and answers 201, with location and an etag of \"1\". Sending the same definition again is safe: it answers 200 with change: \"unchanged\". A different definition under an existing id answers 409 template_exists; use PUT or PATCH to change a template.  id string Required. 1 to 64 lower-case letters, digits, - and _, starting with a letter or dig", "k": "act_threshold answers_only audio change description details etag extensions from full id image location max_questions metadata modalities model models name none note options questions retention_days settings skip state storage temperature text variable_unreferenced variables video warnings", "f": "extensions from id metadata modalities model name note questions retention_days settings state storage variables"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#clone-a-template", "h": "Clone a template", "x": "from copies a definition to start from. {\"template\": \"<reference>\"} copies another template's version, which is how you adapt a read-only starter template. {\"decision\": \"dec_...\"} builds a template from a stored decision: its questions, model and request settings become the template's, and its situation becomes variables (one per field of an object situation, or a single message variable for text)", "k": "from message", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#the-template-object", "h": "The template object", "x": "id, name, description As saved.  origin user, or builtin for starter templates.  archived Hidden from the library; still callable.  metadata, storage, retention_days The head settings.  aliases Alias to version number. latest is always present.  examples_revision, examples_count The test examples: how many, and a counter that rises with every change to them.  version, note, content_hash The versio", "k": "aliases archived builtin change content_hash created created_at description examples_count examples_revision extensions id last_used_at latest metadata metadata_only modalities model name new_version note origin questions required retention_days sensitive settings state storage trusted unchanged updated_at user variables version warnings", "f": "aliases archived change created_at examples_revision id metadata modalities origin version"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#variables", "h": "Variables", "x": "Each variable has a type and optional settings. A variable is required unless it has a default or \"required\": false. A default is used whenever the value is left out, so a variable with one always reads \"required\": false.  string min_length, max_length (default 20,000; at most 200,000), pattern, enum, format ( date or date-time) Text.  integer, number minimum, maximum; enum for integers A number. ", "k": "audio boolean choice date date-time default description enum example false format image integer json max_bytes max_items max_length maximum min_items min_length minimum multi number options pattern rank schema sensitive string true trusted type video", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#the-state", "h": "The state", "x": "state is the situation the model reads, written with {{variable}} placeholders. It can be text or JSON.  A placeholder that is a whole JSON value, such as \"{{open_invoices}}\", keeps the variable's type: a number stays a number.  A placeholder inside longer text is written as text. \\{{ writes a literal {{.  An optional variable that was not sent removes its key from a JSON state, and becomes empty ", "k": "state", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#extensions", "h": "Extensions", "x": "extensions decides how much one call may change the template.  questions true Whether callers may add their own questions.  max_questions 16 How many extra questions one call may add.  options [] Questions that accept new options in add_options: a list of keys, or true for all. Only choice, multi, rank and number questions qualify.  skip [] Questions callers may leave out with skip: a list of keys", "k": "add_options choice extensions max_questions multi number options questions rank skip true", "f": "max_questions options questions skip"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#how-settings-are-chosen", "h": "How settings are chosen", "x": "Each question's act threshold and temperature come from the first of these places that sets them, from most specific to least:  1 request.questions The decision request's settings.questions.<key>.  2 request The decision request's settings.  3 template.models.<model>.questions The template's settings.models.<model>.questions.<key>.  4 template.models.<model> The template's settings.models.<model>.", "k": "request request.questions settings settings.sources studio template template.questions", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#retrieve-a-template", "h": "Retrieve a template", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id} Returns a template with its latest version's definition, and an etag header holding the latest version number.  version A version number or alias, to read that version's definition instead.  include compatibility adds the model check.  include_deleted true to read a deleted template whose decisions are still in history.  The response is shortened; the full object has ", "k": "compatibility etag include include_deleted true version", "f": "include include_deleted version"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#list-templates", "h": "List templates", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates Returns the library, most recently changed first. Each item carries a summary (its question keys, variable names, model and modalities) instead of the full definition.  origin user or builtin.  q Words in the id, name or description.  include_archived true to list archived templates too.  compatible_with A model id: only templates that model can run.  metadata.<key> Exact ", "k": "after builtin compatible_with definition include include_archived limit origin summary true user", "f": "&lt;key&gt; compatible_with include include_archived limit origin q"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#create-or-replace-a-template", "h": "Create or replace a template", "x": "PUT /v1/studio/templates/{id} Makes the template match the body. It creates the template when it does not exist ( 201 ). Otherwise it saves a new version when the definition differs, and does nothing when it is the same, so a setup script or CI job can run it on every deploy. Definition fields you leave out reset to their defaults; head fields you leave out keep their values.  Body The same fields", "k": "base_version change created id If-Match metadata_only new_version unchanged", "f": "base_version If-Match"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#update-a-template", "h": "Update a template", "x": "PATCH /v1/studio/templates/{id} Changes part of a template with a merge patch: objects merge key by key, null removes a key, and anything else replaces. Here the patch adds a question to the existing three and lowers the act threshold, keeping the per-question threshold that was already there. A changed definition saves a new version; a change to head fields only saves none.  id, version, aliases ", "k": "aliases archived base_version current_version id If-Match null support-triage version", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#list-versions", "h": "List versions", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/versions Returns a template's versions, newest first. Each carries its note, the aliases pointing at it, and changes: how it differs from the version before. Add include=definition to get each definition too; limit and after (a version number) page through long histories.  base_version The version the edit started from.  source How it was saved: api, clone, restore or", "k": "after api base_version builtin changes changes.class changes.questions changes.summary clone limit restore source true", "f": "base_version breaking_for_callers class latest_callers_7d questions source summary"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#change-classes", "h": "Change classes", "x": "created The first version.  breaking A question was removed or changed type, a scale's number of levels changed, options were removed, or a variable was removed, retyped, made required or narrowed (it now refuses values it accepted: fewer allowed values, a lower maximum, a higher minimum, a new pattern). Results before and after are not directly comparable.  extended Questions, options, optional v", "k": "added added_options breaking created extended identical incomparable options_changed removed removed_options settings_only text_changed wording", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#retrieve-a-version", "h": "Retrieve a version", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/versions/{n} Returns one version with its full definition. n is a version number, an alias such as production, or latest. Versions of a deleted template stay readable while its decisions are in history", "k": "latest production", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#save-a-version", "h": "Save a version", "x": "POST /v1/studio/templates/{id}/versions Saves a complete definition as the next version. Unlike PATCH, nothing is merged: fields you leave out reset to their defaults. Send base_version (or If-Match) to refuse the save when someone else saved first. The response is the new version object, with x-basal-version-created: true; an identical definition saves nothing and returns the current version with", "k": "base_version false If-Match", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#see-what-changed-between-versions", "h": "See what changed between versions", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/versions/{n}/diff Compares version n with the version before it, or with ?against=<n>. The result has the change class, each question's comparability, changed variables and settings (by path), the model and modalities before and after, a plain summary, and changes: JSON Patch operations that turn one definition into the other", "k": "changes", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#restore-a-version", "h": "Restore a version", "x": "POST /v1/studio/templates/{id}/versions/{n}/restore Saves an old version's definition as a new version, so history stays a straight line and nothing is rewritten. The body may carry a note; it defaults to \"Restore version n\".  The response is shortened. Version 4 has the same content_hash as version 2, because its definition is the same", "k": "content_hash note", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#point-an-alias-at-a-version", "h": "Point an alias at a version", "x": "PUT /v1/studio/templates/{id}/aliases/{alias} DELETE /v1/studio/templates/{id}/aliases/{alias} An alias is a name for a version, such as production or staging. Callers that send support-triage@production follow it, so you can save and test a new version, then move every caller to it in one call, and move them back just as fast. Alias names are 1 to 32 lower-case letters, digits, - and _, starting ", "k": "DELETE latest previous_version production PUT staging version", "f": "version"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#archive-a-template", "h": "Archive a template", "x": "POST /v1/studio/templates/{id}/archive POST /v1/studio/templates/{id}/unarchive Archiving hides a template from the library without breaking anyone. Decisions that use it still run, with a template_archived warning so callers notice. Saving a new version of an archived template answers 409 template_archived; unarchive it first. Both calls return the template object.  The response is shortened to t", "k": "template_archived", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#delete-a-template", "h": "Delete a template", "x": "DELETE /v1/studio/templates/{id} Deleting breaks every caller that uses the template, so it asks you to repeat the id.  confirm Required. The template id again. Without it the call answers 400 confirmation_required.  history keep (the default) or delete.  With history=keep, the template's decisions stay in history, along with the versions they used ( versions_kept ), so you can still read and filt", "k": "confirm decisions_deleted delete history keep versions_kept", "f": "confirm history"}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#variables-as-json-schema", "h": "Variables as JSON Schema", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/schema Returns the variables of a version ( ?version=, latest by default) as a JSON Schema, ready for form builders, validators and code generators. Studio-specific details travel in x-basal- keys: the template reference, its questions, its extensions, and which variables are sensitive or are media", "k": "x-basal-", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#check-which-models-can-run-a-template", "h": "Check which models can run a template", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/compatibility Checks every model the studio knows against a version ( ?version=, latest by default), before anything loads. Each model has ok, its status ( loaded, loading, downloaded, not_downloaded ), the problems that stop it, and notes.  Problems are what stops a model: question types or option counts it cannot handle, too many questions, or a required file it can", "k": "context_too_small downloaded loaded loading not_downloaded notes ok problems state_may_be_truncated status", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#a-templates-history-and-statistics", "h": "A template's history and statistics", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/decisions GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/stats These are List decisions and Decision statistics fixed to one template, and take the same parameters (plus version to pick versions). Statistics group by version by default, and each question says how comparable it is with the first version listed ( reference ), so you can tell a real change in answers from a change in the", "k": "reference version", "f": ""}, {"t": "Templates", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/templates.html#compare-two-versions-over-history", "h": "Compare two versions over history", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/compare Compares how two versions actually answered. For each question it gives each version's answer spread, certainty, act rate and accuracy, and, where both versions saw the same input, how often they agreed and which answers flipped. Inputs are paired by their variables ( paired_by: \"variables_hash\" ), or by the whole input for templates without variables.  versio", "k": "created_after created_before model operational paired.flips paired.sample versions", "f": "created_after model versions"}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html", "h": "", "x": "Each template has its own set of test examples. An example holds the same input a decision would (variables, or a situation for a template without variables, plus any media) and the expected answer to some or all of its questions. The template's Examples tab in the app shows the same data.  The set is revisioned. Every add, edit or removal raises the template's examples_revision by one and never overwrites the old rows, so you can always read the exact set a past evaluation used. Starter templates come with one example each", "k": "examples_revision", "f": ""}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#add-a-test-example", "h": "Add a test example", "x": "POST /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples Adds one example. The input is checked against the template's latest version exactly as a decision would be, and each expected answer must fit its question. Adding an input that is already in the set answers 409 example_exists with the existing example's id in existing_id.  variables object The template's variables.  state string, object or array The situati", "k": "calibration existing_id expected from_decision media note regression split state tags test variables", "f": "expected from_decision media note split state tags variables"}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#from-a-decision-in-history", "h": "From a decision in history", "x": "The quickest way to grow a useful set is to promote decisions a person has already checked. from_decision copies the decision's input, and its feedback becomes the expected answers. The decision must have been stored in full, without redactions and without sensitive variables, which history never keeps. For a template with variables, it must also have been made with this template, so its variables", "k": "from_decision", "f": ""}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#list-test-examples", "h": "List test examples", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples Returns the current examples in the order they were added, with the set's revision.  revision Read the set as it was at an earlier revision.  tag Only examples with this tag.  split test or calibration.  labelled true for examples with at least one expected answer, false for those without.  q Text in the input.  limit, after Up to 1,000 per page (default 100)", "k": "after calibration false labelled limit revision split tag test true", "f": "labelled limit q revision split tag"}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#retrieve-a-test-example", "h": "Retrieve a test example", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples/{eid} Returns one example as it is now, or as it was at an earlier set revision with ?revision=. An example keeps its id across edits; each edit is a new row with a new revision", "k": "revision", "f": ""}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#update-a-test-example", "h": "Update a test example", "x": "PATCH /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples/{eid} Changes an example by writing a new revision of it; the old revision stays readable. variables and expected merge with what is there (send null to remove a key); state, media, tags, split and note replace.  Reading the same example with ?revision=1 still returns it as first added, with two expected answers and two tags", "k": "expected media note null split state tags variables", "f": ""}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#retire-a-test-example", "h": "Retire a test example", "x": "DELETE /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples/{eid} Removes an example from the current set. Past revisions of the set still include it, so earlier evaluations stay reproducible", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#import-test-examples", "h": "Import test examples", "x": "POST /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples/import Adds up to 5,000 examples in one call. Each item takes the same fields as Add a test example. Items that fail do not stop the others: the response counts what was created and lists each failure with its position in the list and the error.  examples array Required. 1 to 5,000 example objects", "k": "examples", "f": "examples"}, {"t": "Test examples", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/examples.html#export-test-examples", "h": "Export test examples", "x": "GET /v1/studio/templates/{id}/examples/export Downloads the current examples as JSON Lines, one example object per line, in a file named after the template. To copy examples to another studio, send their input and expected fields to import; leave out from_decision, which names a decision only this studio has.  Each line is a complete example object; the one above is cut short", "k": "from_decision", "f": ""}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html", "h": "", "x": "All three live under /v1/studio: files at /files, models at /models and settings at /settings. Files and settings can be changed. Models are read-only here; you download, load and eject them on the app's Models page", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html#upload-a-file", "h": "Upload a file", "x": "POST /v1/studio/files Uploads an image, audio or video file once, so decisions can refer to it by id instead of sending the bytes each time. Send it as a multipart form field named file, or as the raw request body with its Content-Type and an X-Filename header (or ?name= ). Files can be up to 200 MB.  image PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF, BMP. audio WAV, MP3, M4A, Ogg, FLAC, WebM. video MP4, MOV, WebM, MKV,", "k": "audio Content-Type expires_at file image video X-Filename", "f": ""}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html#use-files-in-a-decision", "h": "Use files in a decision", "x": "A decision's media is a list of items, each naming one file:  file_id string An id from Upload a file.  data string Or the bytes inline, as a data URL: data:image/png;base64,....  type string image, audio or video. Optional: it is read from the file.  name string Optional, shown in history.  The model must be able to read the media: a text-only model answers 400 model_incompatible before anything ", "k": "audio data file_id image media modalities model_incompatible name store_media type video", "f": "data file_id name type"}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html#retrieve-a-file", "h": "Retrieve a file", "x": "GET /v1/studio/files/{id} GET /v1/studio/files/{id}/content The first returns the file's details, as in the upload response; available is false once the bytes are no longer kept. The second returns the bytes. Images, audio and video are served inline; everything is sent with headers that stop a stored file from running as a page on the studio's address ( content-security-policy: sandbox and x-cont", "k": "available false", "f": ""}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html#delete-a-file", "h": "Delete a file", "x": "DELETE /v1/studio/files/{id} Deletes a file and its bytes at once, even when decisions used it. Privacy comes first: those decisions keep the file's type, size and hash, and show the media as no longer available. They can no longer be rerun", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html#list-models", "h": "List models", "x": "GET /v1/studio/models GET /v1/studio/models/{id} Lists every model the studio knows, downloaded or not, with what it can do. GET /models/{id} returns one; it accepts the studio id, the Hugging Face repository id or the display name.  id, name, maker The id to send as model, and who made it.  modalities What it reads: text, and for some models image, audio, video.  types The question types the mode", "k": "audio context_tokens downloaded id image loaded loading maker max_options max_questions modalities model multi name not_downloaded null number rank revision status text types video", "f": "context_tokens id max_options modalities revision status types"}, {"t": "Files, models and settings", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/resources.html#read-the-settings", "h": "Read the settings", "x": "GET /v1/studio/settings PATCH /v1/studio/settings GET returns the history and decision settings, and how much space history uses. PATCH changes any of the settings below; send only the ones you want to change, grouped as in the response. storage is read-only.  history.store full The most any decision keeps: full, answers_only or none. Requests and templates can only keep less.  history.retention_d", "k": "answers_only blob_bytes db_bytes decisions fail full GET history.keep_labelled history.max_storage_gb history.on_store_error history.retention history.retention_days history.store history.store_media keep_labelled none null PATCH pending_deletion read_only serve storage store_errors true", "f": "default_act_threshold keep_labelled max_storage_gb notice_acknowledged_at on_store_error retention_days store store_media"}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html", "h": "", "x": "These are the studio's management endpoints, like loading and ejecting models. Requests that change something need the header X-Basal-Client: 1, which keeps other websites from using the studio through your browser. Training needs a supported GPU; on other computers the endpoints answer, and capabilities says why training is off", "k": "capabilities", "f": ""}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#check-what-this-computer-can-train", "h": "Check what this computer can train", "x": "GET /api/training/capabilities Whether training is available here, on which device, and which models could be trained. experimental is true for a GPU that trains only after Allow experimental training is turned on; reason says why when enabled is false.  models lists every model, shortened here to two. score orders the recommendation; train_gb is the memory training takes; eta_minutes is filled in", "k": "enabled eta_minutes experimental models reason score train_gb", "f": ""}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#upload-examples", "h": "Upload examples", "x": "POST /api/training/datasets Send a file of examples as the multipart field file, in any format the Train page reads: CSV, tab-separated text, JSON lines or the standard training format, up to 200 MB. Optionally send questions, a JSON object of question definitions keyed like the answers, to set the wording or the options yourself.  The response is the import report: the questions found, how often ", "k": "error file info level message models preview questions report.problems warning", "f": ""}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#start-a-training", "h": "Start a training", "x": "POST /api/training/jobs dataset_id required from the upload. model_id optional the model to train; the recommended one when left out. name optional the trained model's name in the model list. The training starts in its own process and the call returns at once. One training runs at a time on a computer; a second one is accepted and waits its turn ( state waiting ).  A model that can't learn these e", "k": "dataset_id model_id name state waiting", "f": "dataset_id model_id name"}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#follow-a-training", "h": "Follow a training", "x": "GET /api/training/jobs/{id} state queued, waiting, running, paused, done, cancelled or failed. stage, text what it is doing, in words: check, data, load, baseline, train, check_result, save, verify. progress 0 to 1. step, total_steps, eta_seconds while training. curve the practice score on examples it isn't learning from, at each check. baseline_accuracy the original model's score on the test exam", "k": "accepted baseline baseline_accuracy cancelled check check_result collapsed curve data done eta_seconds evaluate failed finetune_id forgot improved load message not_improved notes outcome parity_failed paused progress queued result running save showcase stage state step text total_steps train verify waiting", "f": "baseline_accuracy curve notes progress result stage state step"}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#pause-continue-cancel-or-delete", "h": "Pause, continue, cancel or delete", "x": "POST /api/training/jobs/{id}/{action} action is pause (stops after the current step, keeping what it learned), resume (starts a paused, cancelled or failed training again, from where it stopped when it was paused or cancelled mid-training), cancel (stops and keeps nothing) or delete (stops it if it runs and removes its folder). The response is the training's current state; a pause or cancel takes ", "k": "action cancel cancelled delete pause paused resume state", "f": ""}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#list-trained-models", "h": "List trained models", "x": "GET /api/finetunes Every trained model on this computer, with its record: the model it was trained from, the LoRA settings, the fitted temperature, accuracy before and after on held-out examples and on general questions, the size of each part of the data, and the result of the saved-model check. The same models appear in GET /v1/studio/models and /v1/models under their ids.  Each entry also carrie", "k": "device example versions", "f": ""}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#export-and-import", "h": "Export and import", "x": "GET /api/finetunes/{id}/export The trained model as one .zip: manifest.json, lora.json, lora.safetensors and, when its head was trained, head.safetensors. Local paths are left out. The model it was trained from isn't included; it downloads on the other computer like any other model.  POST /api/finetunes/import Adds a .zip exported from any studio, sent as the multipart field file. The studio accep", "k": "file head.safetensors lora.json lora.safetensors manifest.json", "f": ""}, {"t": "Training and trained models", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/training.html#use-a-trained-model", "h": "Use a trained model", "x": "A trained model is a model: name its id in \"model\" in any decision format, or make it a template's default model. See Teach a model your own decisions", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html", "h": "", "x": "These routes are stateless: a request carries its whole situation and questions, as it would to TypeSafe's hosted API, and the response holds only answers. The studio adds what it can without changing a byte of the response: it records the decision in history and reports that in headers. For templates, stored settings and the act gate, use the studio API, which accepts these same request bodies.  The official SDKs work unchanged: typesafe-sdk for Python and @typesafe-ai/sdk for JavaScript. The studio's tests check both against TypeSafe's published schema", "k": "typesafe-sdk", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#make-a-decision-in-typesafes-format", "h": "Make a decision in TypeSafe's format", "x": "POST /v1/systemone Takes TypeSafe's request and returns TypeSafe's response.  model string Required. A studio model id ( laya ), a Hugging Face repository id, or one of TypeSafe's names such as jev-latest, which route to the most recently loaded model.  state string, object or array Required. The situation.  questions object Required. Question key to question: choice, score and noul, plus the stud", "k": "api_key BASAL_API_KEY choice confidence jev-latest laya legend media metadata model multi noul number probabilities questions rank score settings settings.act_threshold state store temperature x-typesafe-request-id", "f": "media model questions settings state"}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#get-the-studios-extra-answer-fields", "h": "Get the studio's extra answer fields", "x": "Send X-Basal-Extensions: 1 to add the studio's fields to each answer: probabilities for every type, the label in decision, top_probability, any model_extras the model reports (Laya, for example, reports its own act probability), and the response-level latency_ms, wall_ms, passes and notes. Without the header the response is exactly TypeSafe's, and the official SDKs ignore fields they do not know. ", "k": "damaged decision latency_ms model_extras notes passes probabilities top_probability urgency wall_ms wants", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#file-a-call-under-a-template", "h": "File a call under a template", "x": "Wire calls are stored in history like any other decision, without a template. If a call sends exactly the questions of a template version, the header X-Basal-Template: support-triage@2 files it under that version, so it appears in the template's history and statistics. The studio checks the claim: when the questions differ from the version's, the call is still answered and stored, but without the ", "k": "answers_only attributed full metadata mismatch none request.json template_mismatch x-basal-decision-id X-Basal-Metadata X-Basal-Store x-basal-stored X-Basal-Template x-basal-template-status x-basal-warning", "f": "X-Basal-Metadata X-Basal-Store X-Basal-Template"}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#list-models-in-typesafes-format", "h": "List models in TypeSafe's format", "x": "GET /v1/models Returns models in TypeSafe's shape: downloaded and loaded models, led by jev-latest, an alias for the most recently loaded one. The studio API's List models gives more detail.  The response is shortened to three of its entries", "k": "jev-latest", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#gateway-formats", "h": "Gateway formats", "x": "The same decision is also served in the shapes of two gateways that resell TypeSafe's models, so code written for them can point at the studio. Requests take the same body as /v1/systemone.  POST /api/v1/systemone, POST /api/alpha/decisions OpenRouter's System One and Decisions API Adds id, provider and usage.cost. Errors are {\"error\": {\"code\", \"message\"}}, and invalid requests answer 400.  POST /", "k": "boolean id inputTokens outputTokens probability provider provider_metadata usage.cost", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#openrouter", "h": "OpenRouter", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#vercel-ai-gateway", "h": "Vercel AI Gateway", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#vercel-evaluation-api", "h": "Vercel evaluation API", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#errors-on-these-routes", "h": "Errors on these routes", "x": "Each route keeps its API's own error shape, including for the studio's added failures (a refused cross-site request, an idempotency conflict, a failed save). The studio API's error envelope applies only under /v1/studio.  Invalid request 422 {\"detail\": [...]}, one entry per problem with its location 400 {\"error\": {\"code\", \"message\"}} 400 {\"message\", \"error_type\": \"invalid_request\"}. Unknown model ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "TypeSafe and gateway formats", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/systemone.html#how-these-calls-are-recorded", "h": "How these calls are recorded", "x": "Each call is stored in history with source.format set to typesafe, openrouter, vercel or evaluate, source.surface set to api, and the client read from the user agent (the official SDKs are recognised by name). Response bodies are identical whether or not the call was stored. Filter them with format=typesafe in List decisions, and fetch any of them back in its original shape with ?format=.  The act", "k": "api evaluate openrouter settings.act_threshold source.format source.surface typesafe vercel", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html", "h": "", "x": "This page covers /v1/studio. The TypeSafe and gateway routes keep their own error shapes; see Errors on these routes", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#the-error-envelope", "h": "The error envelope", "x": "type The broad kind, from the HTTP status: invalid_request_error, authentication_error, permission_error, not_found_error, conflict_error, rate_limit_error, api_error or model_error.  code A stable name for the exact problem, such as unknown_variable. Branch on this.  message A sentence for people: what is wrong and how to fix it. Messages may be reworded between releases; codes are not.  param Th", "k": "api_error authentication_error code conflict_error current_version decision_id details example_exists existing_id invalid_request_error message model_error not_found_error null param permission_error rate_limit_error request_id type unknown_variable variables.account_tier version_conflict x-request-id", "f": "code decision_id details message param request_id type"}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#which-errors-to-retry", "h": "Which errors to retry", "x": "400, 404, 412, 413, 415 No. Change the request first.  401, 403 No. Fix the key, the origin or the address.  409 Depends on the code. idempotency_in_progress clears in a moment ( retry-after: 1 ); model_not_loaded clears when the model is loaded; the others need a change.  503, 504 Yes, after a pause. These are model and storage failures; a decision that failed this way was usually stored, with it", "k": "decision_id Idempotency-Key idempotency_in_progress model_not_loaded", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#request-errors-400", "h": "Request errors (400)", "x": "The body, a parameter or a field is not valid. Fix the request and send it again.  invalid_json The body is missing, is not valid JSON, or is not an object. Send a JSON object.  invalid_field A body field has the wrong type or an out-of-range value, such as act_threshold: 1.5, an unknown setting, or \"stream\": true. Read param and message.  missing_field A required field is absent: questions withou", "k": "after answers_only confirm confirmation_required created_after expected file filter_required group_by Idempotency-Key If-Match invalid_expected invalid_field invalid_json invalid_metadata invalid_parameter invalid_reference limit message metadata missing_field param pinned questions rating read_only_field search_unavailable template too_many_items version", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#variables-and-the-situation", "h": "Variables and the situation", "x": "missing_variable A required variable was not sent. Send it; the message describes what it takes.  unknown_variable A variable the template does not declare. Check the spelling; the message suggests the closest name.  invalid_variable A value does not fit its variable: wrong type, not in enum, too long, outside minimum and maximum. Send a valid value; the schema lists the rules.  variables_need_tem", "k": "empty_state enum invalid_variable maximum minimum missing_variable state state_not_allowed state_required unknown_variable variables variables_need_template", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#questions-and-extensions", "h": "Questions and extensions", "x": "unknown_question skip, add_options, settings or extensions name a question that does not exist. Check the key.  question_conflict An extra question uses the key of a template question. Choose another key, or use add_options.  extension_not_allowed The template does not allow extra questions, or new options for this question. Change the template's extensions, or save a new version.  options_not_ext", "k": "add_options extension_not_allowed extensions extensions.max_questions extensions.skip no_questions noul option_conflict options_not_extensible question_conflict requires_template score skip skip_not_allowed too_many_extra_questions unknown_question", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#models-and-media", "h": "Models and media", "x": "model_incompatible The model cannot run this decision. details say why: type_not_supported, too_many_options, too_many_questions or modality_not_supported (media it cannot read). Choose another model; compatibility lists the ones that fit.  modality_not_allowed Media of a type the template does not accept. Add the type to the template's modalities.  model_rejected_input The model's worker refused ", "k": "decision_id details modalities modality_not_allowed modality_not_supported model_incompatible model_rejected_input too_many_options too_many_questions type_not_supported", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#saving-a-template", "h": "Saving a template", "x": "undeclared_variable The state or a question uses {{name}} with no such variable. Declare it, or fix the spelling.  placeholder_not_allowed A placeholder where values must be fixed: option names, number values, or the options of a question type that cannot take them from a variable. Put the variable in an option's description, or use an options variable on a choice, multi or rank question.  media_v", "k": "choice enum invalid_definition media_variable_in_text message modalities modality_not_declared multi options param placeholder_not_allowed rank state undeclared_variable", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#access-errors-401-403-415", "h": "Access errors (401, 403, 415)", "x": "401 missing_api_key The studio requires a key and none was sent. Send Authorization: Bearer <key>.  401 invalid_api_key The key does not match BASAL_API_KEY. Check the key.  403 remote_access_requires_key A remote caller reached history, templates or settings on a studio started without BASAL_API_KEY. Set a key on the studio, or call from its own computer.  403 cross_site_request A web page on ano", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS cross_site_request invalid_api_key missing_api_key template_read_only unsupported_media_type", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#not-found-404", "h": "Not found (404)", "x": "decision_not_found No stored decision has that id. It may have been deleted, removed by retention, or made with store: \"none\".  template_not_found No template has that id, or it was deleted (the message gives the date).  template_version_not_found The version does not exist; the message lists the versions that do.  alias_not_found The template has no such alias; the message lists the aliases it ha", "k": "alias_not_found decision_not_found example_not_found feedback_not_found file_not_found method_not_allowed model_not_found not_found template_not_found", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#conflicts-409-and-412", "h": "Conflicts (409 and 412)", "x": "template_exists POST /templates with an existing id and a different definition. Use PUT or PATCH to save a new version.  template_id_reserved The id belonged to a deleted template whose decisions are still in history. Choose another id, or delete that history.  template_archived A new version for an archived template. Unarchive it first.  example_exists The input is already a test example; existin", "k": "base_version current_version decision_finished example_exists existing_id Idempotency-Key idempotency_in_progress idempotency_key_reused If-Match input_unavailable model model_not_downloaded model_not_loaded PATCH PUT retry-after template_archived template_exists template_id_reserved version_conflict", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#size-413", "h": "Size (413)", "x": "payload_too_large: a file over 200 MB, uploaded or sent inline as a data URL", "k": "payload_too_large", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#model-and-storage-failures-500-503-504", "h": "Model and storage failures (500, 503, 504)", "x": "These happen after a request was accepted. A decision that failed while running is stored with status: \"failed\", so the response carries its decision_id.  503 model_load_failed The model could not load, for example because memory ran out. The app's Models page shows why.  503 model_ejected The model was ejected while the request waited for it.  503 model_crashed The model's worker process failed w", "k": "decision_id error.message fail history_unavailable internal_error model_crashed model_ejected model_load_failed model_load_timeout model_timeout on_store_error store_failed", "f": ""}, {"t": "Errors", "s": "API reference", "u": "api/errors.html#warnings", "h": "Warnings", "x": "Problems that do not stop a request come back in warnings, each {code, message, param}, on decisions, previews and template saves. On the wire formats, their codes are in the x-basal-warning header instead.  unknown_field_ignored The body had a field the endpoint does not know.  store_downgraded The template or the studio keeps less than the request asked for.  state_may_be_truncated The input is ", "k": "history_write_failed metadata metadata_ignored no_model_loaded state_may_be_truncated store store_downgraded store_ignored systemone_request template_archived template_mismatch unknown_field_ignored variable_unreferenced warnings X-Basal-Template x-basal-warning", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#the-parts", "h": "The parts", "x": "Bud Decision Studio has four layers, and a fifth process while a model is being trained. Each one can fail or restart without taking the others down.  The interface ( ui/) is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript modules with no build step. It runs in the desktop app's window or in any browser, and talks to the server over HTTP like any other client.  The studio server ( basal/server.py, FastAPI) serves ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#how-a-decision-flows", "h": "How a decision flows", "x": "Every entry point, from the Playground to the official TypeSafe SDK, goes through the same steps. The stateless wire routes ( /v1/systemone and the gateway formats) and the studio API ( /v1/studio/decisions) differ only in steps 2 and 7.  The request is checked before any handler runs. The middleware in server.py refuses a Host header that is not this machine (a guard against DNS rebinding), write", "k": "api_compat.parse BASAL_API_KEY contract.build_answers decisions.resolve guard.py history.insert Host needs_review server.py", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#one-process-per-model", "h": "One process per model", "x": "Loading a model starts a worker, python -m basal.worker, with the model's id, a free port and the load options, and its own log file, DATA/logs/worker-<id>.log. The worker starts a small web server at once and loads the model in a background thread, reporting each stage (\"Importing the model's code\", \"Warming up\") through GET /health. The server polls every worker's health about once a second; tha", "k": "workers.explain_exit", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#adapters-and-the-answer-contract", "h": "Adapters and the answer contract", "x": "basal/contract.py defines one request format for every model. It accepts the six question types and normalises them into the three that decision models answer natively:  choice, score, noul the same question. multi (pick any) one yes-or-no question per option. rank (put in order) one choice question; options are ordered by probability. number (estimate a number) one score question over the allowed", "k": "build_answers choice gliner2 laya multi noul number rank score", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#models-and-downloads", "h": "Models and downloads", "x": "basal/catalog.py lists the eleven models: their Hugging Face repositories, the adapter that runs them, their limits (question types, options, questions, context length) and the plain-language text the interface shows.  basal/hub.py answers \"what is on disk\" by reading the Hugging Face cache directly, so files downloaded by other tools count. When a models folder is chosen on the System page, it re", "k": "HF_HUB_CACHE", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#templates-and-history", "h": "Templates and history", "x": "Templates are split in two. basal/templates.py is pure logic with no database: it checks a definition, fills in variables, merges a caller's extra questions, picks settings per question, classifies how a new version differs from the last, and exports a JSON Schema. basal/template_store.py stores heads, immutable versions, aliases and test examples, and seeds the 30 starter templates ( builtin/<sce", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#the-interface", "h": "The interface", "x": "The interface is ui/index.html plus ES modules in ui/js/, loaded directly by the browser. app.js draws the shell (sidebar, title bar, status bar) and routes between pages in ui/js/pages/; store.js talks to the server and keeps one shared, regularly refreshed copy of GET /api/state. Model details and published results come from ui/registry.json; each model's examples from ui/js/model-guides.js and ", "k": "app.js store.js X-Basal-Client", "f": ""}, {"t": "Architecture", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/architecture.html#where-things-live", "h": "Where things live", "x": "basal/server.py The FastAPI app: request checks, management endpoints ( /api/... ), the wire routes, the interface. basal/studio_api.py The studio API at /v1/studio, with one error envelope for every problem. basal/studio_docs.py Request bodies and examples for the interactive reference at /docs. basal/api_compat.py The published wire formats: TypeSafe, OpenRouter, Vercel AI Gateway. basal/decisio", "k": "BASAL_DATA dec_... install.sh", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#what-you-need", "h": "What you need", "x": "git, to clone the repository.  uv, the Python package manager the studio installs with. install.sh installs it for you when it is missing. uv also fetches Python 3.12, so you do not need a system Python.  Disk space for PyTorch (a 1.5 GB download for the processor build, 4.4 GB for CUDA 13.0) and the model libraries, plus the models you download (0.6 GB for Julia 1, about 24 GB for Jev-Omni).  Nod", "k": "install.sh", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#clone-and-install", "h": "Clone and install", "x": "install.sh runs installer/engine.py setup, the same installer the desktop app runs with buttons. It describes your computer's processors, asks where models should run, then installs into ./.venv:  Python 3.12, in a new virtual environment.  PyTorch 2.11 for the device you chose: CUDA 12.6, 12.8 or 13.0 (picked from your NVIDIA driver), Metal on Apple Silicon, Intel XPU, ROCm, or the CPU.  The mode", "k": "install.sh requirements-cuda.txt requirements-nodeps.txt requirements.txt", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#see-what-setup-would-do", "h": "See what setup would do", "x": "The installer also answers questions without installing anything. detect describes the computer, with the recommended device marked *; plan shows what one device would install", "k": "detect plan", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#run-the-studio", "h": "Run the studio", "x": "run.sh starts the server from .venv and passes its options through. Open http://127.0.0.1:8420 in a browser; the interface is the same one the desktop app shows. Stop the server with Ctrl+C, which ejects every loaded model first.  --port The port to listen on. Default 8420, or BASAL_PORT. --host Default 127.0.0.1: this computer only. 0.0.0.0 makes the studio reachable from your network; set BASAL_", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY BASAL_PORT run.sh", "f": "--host --port"}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#environment-variables", "h": "Environment variables", "x": "The server reads these when it starts. A checkout keeps its data in ./data; the desktop app sets BASAL_DATA to its own folder.  BASAL_DATA Where the studio keeps its database, settings, logs and media files. Default ./data. BASAL_PORT The port, when --port is not given. Default 8420. BASAL_HOST The address to listen on, when --host is not given. Default 127.0.0.1. BASAL_API_KEY Callers from other ", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY BASAL_AUTH_LOCAL BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS BASAL_DATA BASAL_FAKE_LOAD_SECONDS BASAL_FAKE_MODEL BASAL_HOST BASAL_NO_DOWNLOADS BASAL_PARENT_PID BASAL_PORT BASAL_TEST_MODEL BASAL_TEST_URL fake-decider HF_HOME laya", "f": "BASAL_API_KEY BASAL_AUTH_LOCAL BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS BASAL_DATA BASAL_FAKE_LOAD_SECONDS BASAL_FAKE_MODEL BASAL_HOST BASAL_NO_DOWNLOADS BASAL_PARENT_PID BASAL_PORT HF_HOME"}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#work-without-a-gpu-or-models", "h": "Work without a GPU or models", "x": "BASAL_FAKE_MODEL=1 adds Fake Decider ( fake-decider) to the model list. It loads instantly, reads every kind of media, needs no download, and computes its probabilities from a hash of the situation, the question and each option, so the same request always gets the same answer. The test suites and CI use it to exercise the whole API, history included, without PyTorch.  Run it with a throwaway data ", "k": "fake-decider", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#change-the-interface", "h": "Change the interface", "x": "The interface has no build step. Edit the files in ui/, then reload the page: the server sends them with cache-control: no-cache, so the browser always checks for the newer version. DESIGN.md describes its design system.  The 30 starter templates ( builtin/<scenario>) are generated from the Playground's examples. After changing ui/js/examples.js or ui/js/model-guides.js, regenerate basal/builtin_t", "k": "DESIGN.md", "f": ""}, {"t": "Run from source", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/source.html#development-dependencies", "h": "Development dependencies", "x": "The test tools are not part of the engine install. Add them to .venv from requirements-dev.txt: pytest, jsonschema, PyYAML and the official TypeSafe Python SDK ( typesafe-sdk 0.7.2) for the conformance test. The JavaScript SDK test needs npm install in tests/js, and the end-to-end check needs Playwright with Chromium.  What each suite covers and how to run it: Testing", "k": "requirements-dev.txt typesafe-sdk", "f": ""}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#what-a-new-model-needs", "h": "What a new model needs", "x": "A catalog entry in basal/catalog.py. Always.  An adapter in basal/adapters/<family>_adapter.py, registered in basal/adapters/__init__.py, when the model's family has none yet.  Its library in requirements.txt, or in requirements-nodeps.txt when its declared dependencies conflict with the rest, when the adapter imports a package the studio does not install yet.  Details and published results in ui/", "k": "requirements-nodeps.txt requirements.txt", "f": ""}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#the-catalog-entry", "h": "The catalog entry", "x": "Each model is one ModelSpec in the CATALOG list. The runtime uses the facts; the interface shows the plain-language text. The entry for Laya:  id The stable local id, also the model value in API requests. Never change it once released. name, maker What the interface shows. adapter The adapter key in basal/adapters/__init__.py. repo The Hugging Face repository, with optional include and exclude fil", "k": "adapter audio badge base CATALOG choice context_tokens DEVICE DTYPE exclude fast good_for headline_metric heavy id image include instant languages license likes load_options maker max_options max_questions memory_gb modalities model ModelSpec moderate name needs_gpu noul one_pass params repo score size_gb speed summary tagline text True types video watch_out", "f": "adapter base context_tokens id load_options max_options modalities name needs_gpu one_pass params repo speed tagline types"}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#the-adapter", "h": "The adapter", "x": "An adapter runs inside a worker process and owns exactly one loaded model. It subclasses Adapter from basal/adapters/base.py and implements two methods:  load() Load the weights onto self.device. Report progress with self.stage(\"Loading the encoder\", 0.3); the text appears in the interface while the model loads. decide(x) Answer one request. Return a DecideOutput whose probs holds, for each questi", "k": "Adapter DecideOutput keys NOUL_ALIASES probs self.device true x.questions yes", "f": ""}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#what-the-adapter-receives", "h": "What the adapter receives", "x": "decide gets a DecideInput. Its questions are already normalised: pick-any, put-in-order and estimate-a-number questions arrive as noul, choice and score questions, and the studio recomposes the answers afterwards. Your adapter only ever sees three types.  x.request The validated request, including state as the caller sent it (a string, object or list). x.state_text The state rendered as text, for ", "k": "choice contract.build_answers decide DecideInput DecideOutput extras input_tokens notes noul passes probs q.descriptions q.id q.instructions q.keys q.labels q.type score state ValueError x.media x.request x.state_text", "f": "id instructions keys labels media request state_text type"}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#register-it", "h": "Register it", "x": "Add the module to MODULES in basal/adapters/__init__.py, keyed by the name your catalog entry uses in adapter=. Each adapter module ends with ADAPTER = <YourClass>. Modules are imported only when a worker loads a model of that family, so a library one model needs is never imported for the others.  Put the library in requirements.txt. When its package metadata pins versions that conflict with the r", "k": "MODULES requirements-nodeps.txt requirements.txt", "f": ""}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#show-it-in-the-interface", "h": "Show it in the interface", "x": "The Models page reads ui/registry.json: under models.<id>, the maker's logo, links, parameter count, architecture, base model, training, and a list of benchmarks, each with the suite, metric, the model's and Jev's figures, the conditions and the source. Publishers' numbers are labelled as theirs; write what the source says, including its caveats.  The Playground reads ui/js/model-guides.js: one li", "k": "benchmarks", "f": ""}, {"t": "Add a model", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/adding-a-model.html#test-it", "h": "Test it", "x": "Load and ask. Start the studio, download the model on the Models page, and press Decide in the Playground. The worker's log ( View log, or DATA/logs/worker-<id>.log) shows each loading stage and any error.  Smoke test it through the API. scripts/smoke.py <id> loads the model, asks a support ticket's three questions (pick one, a scale, yes or no) three times, prints the answers, the latency and the", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#the-parts", "h": "The parts", "x": "basal/training/dataformat.py the standard training format, the importers for tables and Evaluate files, quality checks, and the grouped, stratified train/calibration/test split. No PyTorch. basal/training/families/base.py the contract every family meets ( FamilyTrainer, Unit, Recipe ), LoRA injection, delta export and attach. basal/training/families/<family>.py one plugin per family: julia, laya, ", "k": "api.py clm FamilyTrainer gliner intern jev_omni julia kev laya lev probe.py Recipe Unit", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#the-family-contract", "h": "The family contract", "x": "A family plugin turns examples into units (one forward pass: one question for models that answer one at a time, or a whole example for models that answer every question in one pass) and returns, for each question in a unit, differentiable log-probabilities over the question's options in the studio's key order. Everything else (the loss, replay, evaluation, calibration, the gate) is written once in", "k": "clm_pairs gliner_tasks intern_request jev_question julia_row kev_record laya_questions lev_questions", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#the-recipes", "h": "The recipes", "x": "Julia 1 LoRA r16 on Wqkv, Wo, Wi; head frozen 5e-5 at 1e-4, or with the head trained, it forgot general decisions. Laya (all three) LoRA r16 on Wqkv, Wo, Wi; head frozen 1e-4 activation checkpointing when the fast path doesn't fit in free memory. GLiNER2.5 Decide LoRA r16 on DeBERTa's attention and dense layers, plus the classifier 1e-4 fp32. Kev 0.5B, Kev 4B a new LoRA on the released targets, ov", "k": "Wi Wo Wqkv", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#a-jobs-life", "h": "A job's life", "x": "The studio starts python -m basal.training.job <job folder>. That process is a supervisor: it waits its turn in the machine-wide queue (a lock file in ~/.cache/bud-decision-studio/ with first-come tickets beside it), then runs the engine in a child process and watches it. If the engine's main thread is silent for 15 minutes (30 while loading or verifying), the supervisor records its stacks and sta", "k": "cancel device.profile events.jsonl finetunes.load_adapter pause predictions.json result.json resume.pt stacks.log status.json", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#the-stored-delta", "h": "The stored delta", "x": "finetunes.refresh() turns every manifest into a catalog entry: the released model's entry with the fine-tune's id, name and temperatures, so downloads, load options and limits follow the released model. A worker loads the released model, then attach injects the LoRA unmerged and loads the head; merging into bf16 weights would round the change away. finetunes.calibrate applies the fitted temperatur", "k": "attach finetunes.calibrate", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#run-a-training-from-the-command-line", "h": "Run a training from the command line", "x": "--override changes any Recipe field. --no-replay and --no-parity exist for experiments. An accepted fine-tune is saved to DATA/finetunes/ and appears in a running studio's model list on its next refresh. BASAL_TRAIN_LOCK names another lock file, so a small development run can go beside a long one; the studio never sets it", "k": "BASAL_TRAIN_LOCK Recipe", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#add-a-family", "h": "Add a family", "x": "Write basal/training/families/<family>.py with a FamilyTrainer subclass and register it in families/__init__.py under the adapter's key.  Move the adapter's input building into a function that decide() and the plugin both call.  Add tests/training/test_<family>.py: a tiny random model with the real tokenizer that is scored, trained two steps, exported, attached to a fresh adapter, and must answer ", "k": "FamilyTrainer manager.py result.json SEC_PER_Q TRAIN_GB", "f": ""}, {"t": "The trainer", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/trainer.html#tests", "h": "Tests", "x": "tests/training/ holds the trainer's tests. The data format, metrics, gate, device policy, queue and supervisor need no PyTorch and run in CI; each family's contract test needs PyTorch and the model's library. scripts/e2e_train.py drives the Train page in a browser from the example file to an answer in the Playground. See Testing", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#where-the-data-lives", "h": "Where the data lives", "x": "The desktop app keeps everything in its application-data folder: the engine it installed ( engine-env/ ), the studio's data ( data/ ), and the server's log ( logs/studio.log ). A source checkout uses ./data inside the checkout. Either way, BASAL_DATA points the server at another folder.  Desktop app on Linux ~/.local/share/ai.bud.decisionstudio/data. Desktop app on macOS ~/Library/Application Supp", "k": "BASAL_DATA config.json HF_HOME models_dir", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#what-is-in-the-data-folder", "h": "What is in the data folder", "x": "studio.db History, templates, versions, test examples, feedback and settings. studio.db-wal and studio.db-shm belong to it while the studio runs. backups/ A copy of studio.db taken before each upgrade changes it; the newest three are kept. blobs/sha256/ Images, audio and video attached to decisions and examples, one file per distinct content. secret A random key, created on first use, for the fing", "k": "activity.jsonl.imported config.json conformance.json conformance.log download_queue.json hub_meta.json secret settings.json studio-port studio.db studio.db-shm studio.db-wal", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#the-database", "h": "The database", "x": "studio.db is SQLite in WAL mode (write-ahead logging, which lets readers keep reading while a write goes on). The server holds one writer connection behind a lock and opens a read-only connection per thread. A decision is written in one transaction of a few milliseconds. Model workers never open the file. On Linux and macOS the file and its companions are readable by your user only.  Times are sto", "k": "api_keys audit_events batches blobs decision_answers decision_bodies decision_fts decision_media decision_metadata decision_secrets decisions eval_runs example_media examples feedback file_... files idempotency_keys production question_sets schema_migrations settings studio.db template_aliases template_questions template_versions templates usage_counters workspace_id ws_local", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#migrations-and-upgrades", "h": "Migrations and upgrades", "x": "The schema changes only through numbered files in basal/migrations/: 0001_init.sql creates the tables, 0002_import_activity.py imports the Activity log of earlier versions, and 0003_search.py adds full-text search when SQLite supports it. At startup the studio applies the ones not yet recorded, in order, each in its own transaction, and records each with a SHA-256 checksum of its file (line ending", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#storage-levels", "h": "Storage levels", "x": "Each decision is stored at one of three levels. The most private of three wins: what the request asks for ( \"store\" or X-Basal-Store ), the template's storage, and the studio's history.store setting. When the result is more private than the request asked, the response carries a store_downgraded warning.  full Everything: variables, the situation, answers, raw probabilities, settings, media files (", "k": "answers_only full history.store none sensitive storage store_downgraded X-Basal-Store", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#media-files", "h": "Media files", "x": "Images, audio and video are stored by content: a file's bytes are named after their SHA-256 hash, in blobs/sha256/ under a folder named for the hash's first two characters, so the same photo sent a hundred times is stored once. Each upload still gets its own random id ( file_... ); an id derived from the content would let one caller find out that another holds a given file. Files are limited to 20", "k": "answers_only decision_media file_... none sensitive", "f": ""}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#retention", "h": "Retention", "x": "The retention sweeper runs 60 seconds after the studio starts, then every hour, and straight after you change retention_days, max_storage_gb or keep_labelled. It deletes decisions older than the retention period, in batches, so new decisions keep saving while it works.  history.retention_days 30 Delete decisions older than this. 0 keeps them forever. A template's own retention_days overrides it fo", "k": "full history.keep_labelled history.max_storage_gb history.retention_days history.store_media keep_labelled max_storage_gb retention_days true", "f": "keep_labelled max_storage_gb retention_days store_media"}, {"t": "Data and storage", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/storage.html#back-up-and-move-your-data", "h": "Back up and move your data", "x": "The data folder is self-contained. To back it up or move it to another computer, copy the whole folder with the studio stopped, and keep secret with studio.db: without it, erasing a sensitive value by value no longer finds the decisions that used it. Models do not need to move with it; download them again on the new computer, or copy the Hugging Face cache separately.  While the studio is running,", "k": "secret studio.db", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html#the-suites", "h": "The suites", "x": "test_templates.py 62 tests of template logic: variable types, substitution, sensitive values, extensions, the settings ladder, version change classes (including narrowed variables), model compatibility, the JSON Schema export, starter templates in sync. test_contract.py 9 tests of answers: per-question temperature, raw probabilities, certainty and the act gate for every type. test_history_store.py", "k": "test_conformance.py test_contract.py test_device_policy.py test_doctor.py test_history_store.py test_installer.py test_job.py test_studio_api.py test_templates.py", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html#run-the-studio-tests", "h": "Run the studio tests", "x": "The command CI runs works from a clean checkout with nothing installed but uv: it fetches Python 3.12 and the few libraries the tests need. From a checkout set up with install.sh, .venv already has them once you add requirements-dev.txt.  test_studio_api.py starts a studio of its own on a free port with a temporary data folder, so it never touches your history or a studio you have open. test_templ", "k": "install.sh requirements-dev.txt test_studio_api.py test_templates.py", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html#api-conformance", "h": "API conformance", "x": "tests/test_conformance.py checks that code written for TypeSafe's Jev works against the studio unchanged. It runs against a live studio, loading the model it is told to use if needed, and compares the studio with the publishers' own sources:  TypeSafe's OpenAPI file ( api.typesafe.ai/openapi.json ): the response schema exactly, extension fields only when asked for, FastAPI-style 422 validation err", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY BASAL_TEST_MODEL BASAL_TEST_URL laya typesafe-sdk", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html#end-to-end-through-the-interface", "h": "End to end, through the interface", "x": "scripts/e2e.py drives the studio in a headless browser the way a person would, against a running studio with real models:  Playground: every downloaded model answers all six question types, and the models that read images answer a receipt photo. Each answer must appear as a chart with no page errors.  Evaluate: a leaderboard over the sample examples with two models.  History and Templates: the dec", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html#other-checks", "h": "Other checks", "x": "scripts/smoke.py Through the API, per model: load, three decisions, latency and memory, eject. --image adds a picture for models that read images. scripts/model-examples.py Each model answers its own signature examples ( ui/js/model-guides.js) as intended. A wrong answer there is a bug. scripts/webkit-sweep.py Every page, dialog and setup screen in WebKit, the engine of Safari and of the macOS and", "k": "safaridriver", "f": ""}, {"t": "Testing", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/testing.html#continuous-integration", "h": "Continuous integration", "x": "Four GitHub Actions workflows run on the repository. The studio tests need no GPU, so they run on every operating system on each change.  studio.yml When basal/, tests/, the examples or the export script change: the studio tests and the trainer's PyTorch-free tests on macOS 14, Windows and Ubuntu 24.04. installer.yml When installer/ or the requirements change: the installer tests, then a real firs", "k": "desktop.yml installer.yml safari.yml studio.yml", "f": ""}, {"t": "Desktop app and releases", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/desktop.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Desktop app and releases", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/desktop.html#what-the-app-does", "h": "What the app does", "x": "The app is deliberately small. It carries three things: the setup screens ( desktop/installer/, plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript), the studio's code, and uv for the target platform. The Rust shell ( desktop/src-tauri/src/main.rs) detects the hardware, runs the installer, and starts and stops the studio server.  First launch The setup screens check the hardware, ask where models should run, and insta", "k": "BASAL_PARENT_PID studio-port taskkill", "f": ""}, {"t": "Desktop app and releases", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/desktop.html#how-the-engine-is-bundled", "h": "How the engine is bundled", "x": "desktop/scripts/stage.mjs runs before every tauri dev and tauri build. It copies:  basal/, ui/, installer/engine.py and the requirements*.txt files into src-tauri/engine/, which the app bundles as a resource;  the brand mark, the Inter font, the icons and the interface's colour tokens into the setup screens, so setup looks like the studio;  uv for the target platform into src-tauri/binaries/bud-uv", "k": "PATH PYTHONHOME PYTHONPATH", "f": ""}, {"t": "Desktop app and releases", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/desktop.html#work-on-the-app", "h": "Work on the app", "x": "To work on the app against a checkout, point it at the checkout's code, Python and data with three variables, so nothing is reinstalled. The setup screens can be previewed in any browser by opening desktop/installer/index.html: ?hw=gb10, ?hw=mac, ?hw=intel or ?hw=cpu simulates a computer, and ?installed=1 shows the launch screen.  BUD_STUDIO_ENGINE Run the studio's code from this folder instead of", "k": "BUD_STUDIO_DATA BUD_STUDIO_DEVICE BUD_STUDIO_ENGINE BUD_STUDIO_PYTHON cpu cuda mps recommended rocm xpu", "f": "BUD_STUDIO_DATA BUD_STUDIO_DEVICE BUD_STUDIO_ENGINE BUD_STUDIO_PYTHON"}, {"t": "Desktop app and releases", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/desktop.html#build-the-installers", "h": "Build the installers", "x": "npx tauri build builds for the computer you run it on. It needs Rust, Node 18 or newer, and on Linux the WebKitGTK 4.1 development packages.  macOS, Apple Silicon macOS .app, .dmg. Windows x64 Windows .msi, -setup.exe. Linux x64 and ARM64 Linux .deb, .rpm, .AppImage. On Linux, tauri build downloads AppImage tools from GitHub and can time out on a slow connection. Download them once into ~/.cache/t", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Desktop app and releases", "s": "Developers", "u": "dev/desktop.html#make-a-release", "h": "Make a release", "x": "A release is a version bump, a tag, and GitHub Actions. The version appears in five files, plus the two lock files that follow them:  basal/__init__.py __version__, shown by the server and recorded with every decision. desktop/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json version, the app's version. desktop/src-tauri/Cargo.toml version. desktop/package.json version. site/docs-src/nav.py VERSION, shown in these docs. ", "k": "__version__ desktop.yml get.ps1 get.sh main VERSION version", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#where-to-look-first", "h": "Where to look first", "x": "A model's log. On the Models page, select the model and choose View log. The same file is logs/worker-<model>.log in the data folder.  The server's log. The desktop app writes it to logs/studio.log in its application-data folder. From a source checkout, it is the terminal running ./run.sh.  A download's log. logs/download-<model>.log in the data folder.  The environment check. From a source checko", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#installing", "h": "Installing", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#macos-says-the-app-is-from-an-unidentified-developer", "h": "macOS says the app is from an unidentified developer", "x": "The macOS build is not code-signed yet. Right-click the app and choose Open once, or install with the one-line command, which avoids the warning", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#windows-smartscreen-warns-about-the-installer", "h": "Windows SmartScreen warns about the installer", "x": "The Windows build is not code-signed yet. Choose More info, then Run anyway, or install from PowerShell, which avoids the warning", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#setup-stops-with-an-error", "h": "Setup stops with an error", "x": "Setup installs Python, PyTorch and the model libraries into the app's own folder; it changes nothing outside it. When a step fails, setup says which one. Press Try again: setup reuses whatever it already installed and continues where it stopped. Details shows the full log, and Copy details copies it for a bug report.  PyTorch could not be installed. Check the internet connection and try again. The", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#the-app-window-is-blank-on-linux", "h": "The app window is blank on Linux", "x": "WebKitGTK's DMA-BUF renderer draws nothing on many NVIDIA systems. The app turns it off by itself ( WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1) unless that variable is already set in your environment. If you set it yourself, set it to 1", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#the-studio-did-not-start", "h": "The studio did not start", "x": "The desktop app shows this, followed by the last lines of the server's log, when the server exits or does not answer within three minutes. The first start after installing imports PyTorch, which can be slow on a cold disk; open the app again. If it fails again, the log lines name the cause; the most common is a missing library, fixed by running setup again ( System page, Run setup again )", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#starting-from-source", "h": "Starting from source", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#run-installsh-first", "h": "Run ./install.sh first", "x": "run.sh found no .venv in the checkout. Run ./install.sh (or install.ps1 on Windows) once", "k": "install.ps1 run.sh", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#address-already-in-use", "h": "address already in use", "x": "Another program is listening on port 8420, most often another studio: the desktop app or a second ./run.sh. Use the one that is running, stop it, or start this one on another port with ./run.sh --port 8421. The desktop app avoids this itself by picking a free port from 8421 to 8440.  To run two studios side by side on purpose, give the second its own data folder and turn its downloads off", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#models", "h": "Models", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#downloads-are-slow", "h": "Downloads are slow", "x": "Hugging Face limits anonymous downloads. Run hf auth login once in a terminal and restart the studio. Downloads run one at a time, smallest first, so a large model waits behind smaller ones.  download of <repository> failed (exit 1); see data/logs/download-<model>.log The download process failed: often the connection or the disk. The log has the reason. Press Download again; files already complete", "k": "BASAL_NO_DOWNLOADS", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#a-model-does-not-load", "h": "A model does not load", "x": "Most load failures are memory. The studio runs each model in its own process and reports why it stopped.  The GPU ran out of memory. Eject another model to free space, then try again. Not enough free GPU memory. Eject other models (sidebar, or Eject all models on the System page) or close other programs using the GPU. The model process was killed, most likely because the system ran out of memory. ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#answers-are-slow-and-the-answer-says-it-ran-on-the-processor", "h": "Answers are slow, and the answer says it ran on the processor", "x": "Running on the processor, about ten times slower: the GPU did not have enough free memory when this model loaded. Eject other models (or close other GPU programs) and load it again.  The model asked for the GPU, but the GPU was full when it loaded, and its library fell back to the processor. The answers are the same, only slower. Free GPU memory, then eject the model and load it again. Until it is", "k": "notes", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#a-call-says-no-model-is-loaded", "h": "A call says no model is loaded", "x": "No model is loaded. Load one on the Models page (or POST /api/models/{id}/load), or name a downloaded model in the request's model field. The request named no model and none is loaded. Name a model in \"model\", or load one. Laya isn't loaded. Load it first (auto-load is off). Load models on demand is off on the System page. Load the model first, or turn it back on. Laya isn't downloaded. Download i", "k": "model", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#run-on-the-processor-or-switch-back-to-the-gpu", "h": "Run on the processor, or switch back to the GPU", "x": "This computer cannot run models on 'cuda'. Run setup again to install support for it. The engine was installed for another device. Setup installs one PyTorch build at a time; run it again and choose the device ( System, then Run setup again, or ./install.sh --device cuda from source). Everything else is kept", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#training", "h": "Training", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#the-train-page-says-training-isnt-available", "h": "The Train page says training isn't available", "x": "Training needs a GPU. This computer runs models on the processor. Training is off on computers without a supported GPU; every model still runs there. If the computer has a GPU, the engine was installed for the processor: run setup again and choose the GPU ( System, then Run setup again, or ./install.sh from source). On Intel Arc and Core Ultra graphics, AMD on Linux, NVIDIA RTX 20 series and Apple", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#the-file-cant-be-used", "h": "The file can't be used", "x": "The review screen lists each problem in plain words; one shown in red stops training until the file is fixed.  Every example in 'label' has the same answer ('spam'). The model needs examples of at least two different answers. Add examples of the other answers. The file has only 12 examples. Add at least 30 (a few hundred works much better). Add examples. Some answers to 'team' don't match its opti", "k": "text", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#training-stops-or-waits", "h": "Training stops or waits", "x": "Waiting for another training to finish One training runs at a time on a computer. It starts by itself; Cancel removes it from the queue. Other programs left only 2.8 GB of memory free. It will continue by itself when there is room. Training waits instead of letting the computer swap. Close programs or eject models; it continues by itself 30 seconds after memory is free, and gives up with a resume ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#the-result-wasnt-kept", "h": "The result wasn't kept", "x": "The page names the reason; the original model is unchanged. See When the result isn't kept for what helps, or choose Teach another model instead. The saved model did not answer exactly like the trained one is a bug: the job folder ( training/jobs/<id> in the studio's data folder) keeps the rejected files and the log; please report it", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#importing-a-trained-model-fails", "h": "Importing a trained model fails", "x": "That file isn't a .zip exported from Bud Decision Studio. Choose the .zip that Export saved. It was trained from 'kev-4b', which this studio doesn't have. Update the studio, then import it again. The model it was trained from isn't in this version's catalog. That .zip doesn't hold a fine-tuned model exported from Bud Decision Studio (unexpected: ...). The archive holds other files; export it again", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#calling-the-api", "h": "Calling the API", "x": "Management requests need the header 'X-Basal-Client: 1' (it protects the studio from other websites). A call to a management endpoint under /api/ that changes something (load, eject, download, settings) came without the header. Add -H 'X-Basal-Client: 1'. Requests from https://example.com may not change this studio. Other websites can't write to it; to allow one, list it in BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS. A w", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY localhost", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#history", "h": "History", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#history-does-not-save-and-the-servers-log-says-why", "h": "History does not save, and the server's log says why", "x": "When history cannot open, decisions keep working and nothing is saved, and the history endpoints answer 503 history_unavailable. The server prints the reason at startup, in its log.  This history was written by a newer version of the studio, so it opens read-only. Update the studio to keep saving decisions. You opened your data with an older studio after using a newer one. Install the newer versio", "k": "install.sh studio.db", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#the-answer-is-correct-but-it-could-not-be-saved-to-history", "h": "The answer is correct, but it could not be saved to history", "x": "A decision came back with this warning ( history_write_failed) when writing to the database failed, usually a full disk. The answer is unaffected. GET /v1/studio/settings lists recent write errors under storage.store_errors. If you would rather get an error than an unsaved answer, set history.on_store_error to fail; the studio then answers 503 store_failed", "k": "fail history.on_store_error history_write_failed storage.store_errors", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#decisions-disappear-from-history", "h": "Decisions disappear from History", "x": "Decisions are kept for 30 days by default, and history is capped at 20 GB, oldest first. Change both on the History page or with PATCH /v1/studio/settings. Pinned decisions and labelled ones (while keep labelled is on) are never removed; see Data and storage", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#a-background-decision-failed-with-interrupted", "h": "A background decision failed with interrupted", "x": "The studio stopped while the decision was queued or running. At the next start it is marked failed with error.code: \"interrupted\" rather than run again, so no request ever runs twice. Send it again, or rerun it from History", "k": "interrupted", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#search-says-it-is-unavailable", "h": "Search says it is unavailable", "x": "Full-text search needs an SQLite build with FTS5, which this one lacks. The q filter needs SQLite's full-text search, which some builds leave out. Every other filter works", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "s": "Help", "u": "troubleshooting.html#still-stuck", "h": "Still stuck", "x": "Report a problem on GitHub with the message, what you did, your operating system and device, and the relevant log: the model's log for a model problem, the server's log for anything else, or setup's Copy details for an install", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#privacy", "h": "Privacy", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#does-anything-i-send-leave-my-computer", "h": "Does anything I send leave my computer?", "x": "No. Models run on your computer, and requests, answers and history stay in the studio's data folder. The studio sends nothing about your decisions anywhere and has no telemetry.  It does use the internet for three things, all to fetch software: installing the engine (Python, PyTorch and the model libraries), downloading the models you choose from Hugging Face, and reading each model's file list, s", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#where-is-my-history-kept-and-how-do-i-delete-it", "h": "Where is my history kept, and how do I delete it?", "x": "In studio.db in the data folder. Delete single decisions or everything matching a filter on the History page, or with DELETE /v1/studio/decisions/{id} and the bulk endpoints. Decisions older than 30 days are removed automatically; you can change the period or keep them forever", "k": "studio.db", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#how-do-i-stop-the-studio-keeping-decisions", "h": "How do I stop the studio keeping decisions?", "x": "At whichever level suits you:  One call: send \"store\": false in the body, or the header X-Basal-Store: 0. TypeSafe's own servers ignore the field, so the same code runs against both.  One template: set the template's storage to none.  Everything: on the History page, choose Nothing for what history keeps, or send {\"history\": {\"store\": \"none\"}} with PATCH /v1/studio/settings.  \"answers_only\" is the", "k": "none sensitive storage", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#hardware", "h": "Hardware", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#do-i-need-a-gpu", "h": "Do I need a GPU?", "x": "No. Every model except Jev-Omni runs on the processor, and small models such as Laya and Julia 1 answer in about a second there. A GPU makes the 4B to 12B models practical. On a computer with an Intel or Apple GPU, Lev runs on the processor, and Jev-Omni, which needs an NVIDIA GPU, is not offered.  NVIDIA GPU (GeForce, RTX, GB10 and DGX Spark, data-centre cards) CUDA 12.6, 12.8 or 13.0, chosen fro", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#how-much-memory-does-a-model-need", "h": "How much memory does a model need?", "x": "The memory each model needs once loaded, as the Models page shows it:  Julia 1, Laya Multilingual, Laya, Laya Typed-Decisions, Kev 0.5B 0.8 to 1.3 GB. GLiNER2.5 Decide 2 GB. Kev 4B, Lev 9.5 GB. Intern-Decision 4B 10 GB. CLM 8B 17 GB. Jev-Omni 26 GB, on an NVIDIA GPU only. Several models can be loaded at once. A model too large for your device is marked Too large here on the Models page and the loa", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#do-i-need-a-gpu-to-train-a-model", "h": "Do I need a GPU to train a model?", "x": "Yes. Teaching a model on the Train page needs an NVIDIA RTX 30 series or newer (including the GB10), or an Apple M2 or newer; Intel Arc and Core Ultra graphics, AMD on Linux, older NVIDIA cards and the M1 work as an experimental option. Trained models then run anywhere their original model runs, including on the processor", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#models", "h": "Models", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#which-model-should-i-start-with", "h": "Which model should I start with?", "x": "Fast English triage, routing and guardrails Laya. Customer messages in other languages Laya Multilingual, or Julia 1 for routing. The best all-round answers, and images Intern-Decision 4B. Careful, well-calibrated answers on long policies Kev 4B. Questions with hundreds of options Lev. An AI agent choosing its next action CLM 8B. The hardest questions, or audio and video Jev-Omni. Each model comes", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#are-the-answers-the-same-as-jevs", "h": "Are the answers the same as Jev's?", "x": "No. These are open models from different makers, trained differently from TypeSafe's Jev; the studio runs them behind Jev's API. The Models page shows each model's published results next to Jev's on the same benchmarks, labelled as the publishers' own figures. Probabilities are comparable across models in shape, but each model's calibration differs; the Evaluate page measures it on your own exampl", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#what-licence-do-the-models-have", "h": "What licence do the models have?", "x": "Each model keeps its own licence. The studio's catalog lists all eleven under Apache-2.0; each model's page in the app links to its Hugging Face card, which is the authority. Weights come from each publisher's Hugging Face repository and are not redistributed by the studio", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#training", "h": "Training", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#do-my-examples-leave-my-computer", "h": "Do my examples leave my computer?", "x": "No. Training runs on this computer's GPU; the file you add is kept in the studio's data folder with the training job, and nothing is uploaded", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#how-many-examples-do-i-need", "h": "How many examples do I need?", "x": "At least 30 answered questions, and a few hundred for a result you can rely on: the studio sets about a third of them aside to test the result, and with fewer than 300 only large improvements can be told apart from luck. Every answer should appear at least ten to twenty times. Teach a model your own decisions has the details", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#can-training-make-the-model-worse", "h": "Can training make the model worse?", "x": "Not one you get to use. The trained model is tested on examples it never saw and on general questions it never trains on, and it is added only if it is better on the first without a clear drop on the second; otherwise the original stays and the page says why. The original model is never changed. Fine-tuning and its safeguards lists the checks and the results on every model", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#can-i-use-a-trained-model-on-another-computer", "h": "Can I use a trained model on another computer?", "x": "Yes. Export on its row of the Train page saves it as one .zip, a few to a few hundred MB; Import a trained model on the other computer's Train page adds it. The model it was trained from must be downloaded there too", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#using-it-from-code", "h": "Using it from code", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#does-my-code-written-for-jev-work", "h": "Does my code written for Jev work?", "x": "Yes, by changing the base URL to http://127.0.0.1:8420. The studio serves TypeSafe's POST /v1/systemone and GET /v1/models with the same request and response shapes, and the official SDKs, typesafe-sdk for Python and @typesafe-ai/sdk for JavaScript, pass every conformance check unchanged. OpenRouter's and Vercel AI Gateway's formats are served too. Responses are exactly TypeSafe's shape unless you", "k": "typesafe-sdk", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#what-is-the-difference-between-v1systemone-and-v1studio", "h": "What is the difference between /v1/systemone and /v1/studio?", "x": "/v1/systemone is stateless and TypeSafe-compatible: you send the situation and questions every time. /v1/studio adds what a local studio can offer: templates you call by name with variables, versions, a decision object that says whether to act, history you can query, feedback and test examples. Both record decisions in history. See the API overview", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#can-it-serve-other-machines", "h": "Can it serve other machines?", "x": "Yes. Start it with --host 0.0.0.0 and set BASAL_API_KEY; callers then send Authorization: Bearer <key>. Without a key, other machines can make decisions but cannot reach history, templates or settings. The studio speaks plain HTTP and has one workspace; to serve beyond a trusted network, put it behind a reverse proxy that adds HTTPS", "k": "BASAL_API_KEY", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#can-a-web-page-call-it-from-the-browser", "h": "Can a web page call it from the browser?", "x": "Only pages you allow. Other websites open in your browser cannot send the studio requests or change anything. List your own web app's origin in BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS when starting the studio", "k": "BASAL_CORS_ORIGINS", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#the-app", "h": "The app", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#how-do-i-update", "h": "How do I update?", "x": "Install the new version over the old one: download it from the latest release, or run the one-line install command again, then quit and reopen the app. The engine, settings, models, templates and history are kept. The app does not update itself yet", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#how-do-i-uninstall-it", "h": "How do I uninstall it?", "x": "Remove the app as you would any other. The engine and your data are in the app's application-data folder ( ai.bud.decisionstudio, listed on Data and storage ); delete it to remove them too. Models are in the Hugging Face cache, ~/.cache/huggingface/hub, which other tools may share, unless you chose another Models folder on the System page; delete them on the Models page before uninstalling, or rem", "k": "ai.bud.decisionstudio", "f": ""}, {"t": "FAQ", "s": "Help", "u": "faq.html#something-is-not-working", "h": "Something is not working", "x": "Troubleshooting lists the studio's messages with their causes and fixes. If yours is not there, report a problem", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html", "h": "", "x": "", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#unreleased", "h": "Unreleased", "x": "Changes on the main branch that are not in a release yet.  The three Laya models load on Intel GPUs (Core Ultra and Arc, on Windows and Linux). They failed with \"expected scalar type BFloat16 but found Float\": inside mixed precision, PyTorch ran the decision head through a native fast path whose Intel kernel cannot mix precisions, because its check for mixed precision only looks at NVIDIA. The stu", "k": "main", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#030", "h": "0.3.0", "x": "1 October 2026. Teach a model your own decisions, and twelve fixes, one of them for privacy", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#new-teach-a-model-your-own-decisions", "h": "New: teach a model your own decisions", "x": "The Train page fine-tunes any of the eleven models on a spreadsheet of your past decisions, on this computer's GPU. It works out the questions from the file, recommends a model with a time estimate, trains it, and shows the result on examples it never saw, with one it used to get wrong. Use it now opens the new model in the Playground; Compare on Evaluate opens Evaluate with the held-out examples ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#fixes", "h": "Fixes", "x": "A sensitive file is never kept. An image, audio or video variable marked sensitive was stored with the decision: its contents, its file name and its content hash. The model now reads it and History keeps only a keyed hash, as for every other sensitive value. A queued background decision no longer holds inline file data either, and temporary copies are removed even when a decision fails or is cance", "k": "history_write_failed sensitive", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#021", "h": "0.2.1", "x": "30 September 2026. The decisions endpoint is easy to find, and so is what gets saved.  The API page opens on the studio API. POST /v1/studio/decisions comes first, and its quick start runs a starter template. Before, it sat behind a fourth tab and looked missing.  Every decision endpoint says \"Saved to History\", in every format (the studio API, TypeSafe, OpenRouter and Vercel), and the page says h", "k": "x-basal-decision-id", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#020", "h": "0.2.0", "x": "30 September 2026. Templates and history: decisions you make once can be reused, and every decision is kept, so you can see how they went and improve them.  History replaces Activity. Every decision is kept on this computer, from the Playground or from your code through any API format: the situation, the questions, the answers, the model and the timing. Filter by template, model, answer or time; o", "k": "sensitive", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#015", "h": "0.1.5", "x": "30 September 2026. Updates always show.  No stale interface after an update. The studio tells the app window, and any browser, to check for newer scripts and styles every time; unchanged files cost only a quick check. Before, a window could keep running the previous version's files after an update.  The desktop app keeps the same address. When port 8420 was taken, the app picked a random port on e", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#014", "h": "0.1.4", "x": "30 September 2026. Every model comes with its own examples, each run on its model and checked to give the right answers.  Choosing a model shows what it is made for. CLM 8B opens on a web agent choosing its next action, Lev on a question with 77 banking intents, Kev 4B on a refund policy with an exception, Laya Multilingual on a message in Japanese, Jev-Omni on a receipt photo.  In the Playground,", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#013", "h": "0.1.3", "x": "30 September 2026. Start a new decision from scratch.  New, next to Examples (and Blank decision at the top of the Examples menu), clears the situation, questions, attachments and answers, and keeps your model and settings. Undo brings everything back.  A blank Playground shows three steps (describe the situation, add a question, press Decide), ticked off as you go.  Pressing Decide before adding ", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#012", "h": "0.1.2", "x": "30 September 2026. Fixes the \"Choose models to download\" sheet on macOS.  The model list is visible in Safari's engine. On 0.1.1 it collapsed to a thin strip showing half of one row, so you could not tick models. The list and the other scrolling areas now start at their content's height in every browser engine.  Tested in real Safari on macOS 14 and 15 on every interface change, through Apple's sa", "k": "safaridriver", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#011", "h": "0.1.1", "x": "30 September 2026. Fixes first-run setup on macOS.  Setup finishes on macOS. On 0.1.0 it stopped at \"Installing the model libraries\" with error: File not found: /Users/<you>/Library/Application, because uv split a file path at the space in \"Application Support\". Setup now names the PyTorch build directly, with no file path involved. The same could have affected Windows user names with a space.  Se", "k": "", "f": ""}, {"t": "Changelog", "s": "Help", "u": "changelog.html#010", "h": "0.1.0", "x": "30 September 2026. The first release: run open decision models on your own computer.  Eleven models: Julia 1, Laya, Laya Multilingual, Laya Typed-Decisions, Kev 0.5B, Kev 4B, Lev, GLiNER2.5 Decide, Intern-Decision 4B, CLM 8B and Jev-Omni.  Six kinds of question: pick one, rate on a scale, yes or no, pick any, put in order, estimate a number.  Pages: Playground, Models, Evaluate, Activity, API, Lea", "k": "", "f": ""}];
